insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (1, 'Genesis does not name its author. Jewish and Christian tradition holds that it was written by Moses, an Israelite raised in the Egyptian royal court who later led his people out of slavery and served as their prophet and lawgiver. Many scholars instead think the book was woven together over centuries from several older collections of stories and family records. On that view it reached its final form around the time of Israel''s exile in Babylon or shortly after, in the 500s BC.', 'On the traditional view, the first hearers were the Israelites who had escaped Egypt and were living as wandering tribes in the desert, on their way to settle in Canaan, the land later called Israel. Scholars who favour a later date picture Israelites in or just after exile in Babylon, a people who had lost their land, king and temple. In either setting, these were people asking who they were and where they had come from.', 'The stories themselves are set long before they were written down: the families of Abraham, Isaac and Jacob are usually placed somewhere around 2000–1500 BC, moving between Mesopotamia (modern Iraq, Syria and south-east Turkey), Canaan and Egypt. That world was made up of great river kingdoms, small city-states and herding families who travelled with their flocks in search of pasture and water. The surrounding peoples, especially in Babylon, told their own stories of creation and of a great flood. The book was written down either around the 1400s–1200s BC, on the traditional view, or as late as the 500s BC on the main alternative.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (2, 'Jewish and Christian tradition holds that Moses wrote Exodus. He was an Israelite raised in the household of Egypt''s royal family, then spent years as a shepherd in the desert before becoming the leader, lawgiver and prophet who brought his people out of Egypt. Many modern scholars think the book was woven together later from several older written and spoken sources, perhaps reaching its final form around the time of the Babylonian exile (500s BC), while still drawing on very old traditions.', 'On the traditional view, Exodus was first meant for the Israelites who had escaped Egypt and were living as a wandering people in the desert, learning to live as a nation. On the later-compilation view, it was gathered for Israelites centuries afterward, many of them exiled or newly returned home, who needed to remember where they came from.', 'Egypt was the great power of the day, ruled by pharaohs who used forced labour from foreign peoples to build cities and monuments. The date of the escape from Egypt is debated: some place it around 1446 BC, others around 1250 BC in the reign of Ramesses II. After leaving Egypt the Israelites lived as nomads in the harsh Sinai desert, between Egypt and the land of Canaan. Neighbouring peoples worshipped many gods and had their own law codes and sacred shrines.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (3, 'Jewish and Christian tradition holds that Moses wrote Leviticus. He was an Israelite raised in the Egyptian royal court, later a shepherd in the desert, and then the leader who brought his people out of slavery and passed on God''s laws to them. Many modern scholars think the book took its final shape centuries later, gathered by priests from older traditions, perhaps during or soon after the Babylonian exile. Either way, it reads as a manual for worship, largely concerned with the work of priests from the tribe of Levi, which gives the book its name.', 'The book presents itself as instructions given to the Israelites camped at Mount Sinai, newly freed from Egypt, and especially to Aaron and his sons, the first priests. They were a large, wandering people learning how to live and worship together before entering the land of Canaan. If the book was compiled later, its first readers would also have included Israelites around the exile who were trying to keep their worship and identity intact.', 'In the traditional view the setting is the Late Bronze Age, roughly the 1400s or 1200s BC, when Egypt was the great power and Canaan was a patchwork of small city-states. Leviticus picks up just after the end of Exodus, when the Israelites had finished building the tabernacle, or "tent of meeting", a portable tent-shrine where they believed God''s presence lived among them. The whole book covers only about a month at Sinai. Animal sacrifice and rules about ritual "cleanness", meaning fitness to take part in worship, were normal features of religion across the ancient world.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (4, 'Jewish and Christian tradition names Moses as the author of Numbers and the other four books of the Law. Moses was an Israelite raised in the Egyptian royal court who became the prophet and leader who brought Israel out of slavery in Egypt and through the desert. Many scholars instead see the book as gathered from older records, laws and stories over several centuries, reaching its final form around the time of the exile in Babylon or soon after (500s–400s BC).', 'Traditionally, the first hearers were the new generation of Israelites camped on the plains of Moab, east of the Jordan River, about to enter the land of Canaan after their parents'' generation had died in the desert. Many scholars also see the finished book as speaking to later Israelites, possibly exiles in Babylon or those returning home, who were themselves waiting to settle again in the promised land.', 'The book covers roughly forty years of Israel''s life as a wandering people in the desert, from Mount Sinai to the edge of Canaan. The date of the exodus from Egypt is debated, with the two main views placing it in the 1400s BC or the 1200s BC. Canaan was then a patchwork of small walled city-states, often under Egyptian influence, surrounded by neighbouring peoples such as the Edomites, Moabites, Amorites and Midianites. Israel lived in tents, organised by tribes and clans, with the portable worship tent called the tabernacle at the centre of the camp.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (5, 'Deuteronomy presents itself as the farewell speeches of Moses, the leader who brought Israel out of slavery in Egypt and received God''s law at Mount Sinai. Raised in the Egyptian royal court, he spent forty years guiding the people through the wilderness as prophet, lawgiver and judge. Jewish and Christian tradition has long held Moses as its author, though the account of his death in the final chapter was added by someone else. Many scholars think the book drew on old traditions from Moses, but that its core was shaped centuries later by priests or scribes in Judah around the time of King Josiah (about 620 BC), with further additions during or after the exile in Babylon.', 'The speeches are addressed to the Israelites camped on the plains of Moab, just east of the Jordan River, after forty years in the wilderness. Most were the children of those who had left Egypt, and they were about to cross into Canaan without Moses to lead them. If the book took its final shape later, it also spoke to the people of Judah in Josiah''s day and to those later carried into exile in Babylon.', 'On the traditional dating, this is somewhere between about 1400 and 1200 BC, when Canaan was a patchwork of small walled city-states whose local kings answered to Egypt. Israel had just passed the lands of Edom, Moab and Ammon and defeated two Amorite kings, Sihon and Og, east of the Jordan. The book is laid out much like ancient treaties between a great king and a smaller nation. Scholars who favour a later date point to the seventh century BC, when Assyria dominated the region and King Josiah carried out sweeping religious reforms around 622 BC.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (6, 'The book is named after Joshua son of Nun, from the tribe of Ephraim, who served as Moses'' assistant and then led Israel''s army after Moses died. Jewish tradition holds that Joshua wrote most of it himself, with the account of his death added afterward by the priests Eleazar and Phinehas. Many scholars instead think it was gathered and shaped centuries later, drawing on older records, lists and stories, as part of a longer history of Israel that runs from Deuteronomy through Kings.', 'If Joshua wrote it, the first readers were the Israelites who had crossed the Jordan with him and were settling in Canaan, the land promised to their ancestors. If it was shaped later, it was read by Israelites under their kings, or by exiles in Babylon who had lost that same land and were looking back on how it was first received.', 'The events take place in Canaan, roughly modern Israel and the West Bank, either around 1400 BC by the traditional reckoning or around 1250–1200 BC as many scholars suggest. Canaan was a patchwork of small walled cities, each ruled by its own king, loosely under the shadow of Egypt. Its people worshipped many gods, especially the storm god Baal. Archaeologists still debate how the evidence on the ground fits the book''s account of these years.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (7, 'Judges does not name its author. Jewish tradition, recorded in the Talmud (an ancient collection of Jewish teaching), credits the prophet Samuel, the last of Israel''s leaders before the kings. Many scholars instead think the book was gathered from older stories and songs and shaped by later Israelite writers, possibly during the time of the kings or even after the exile.', 'The first readers were Israelites looking back on their nation''s early, chaotic days in the land. The book''s repeated remark that there was no king in Israel suggests they already lived under kings and could compare those times with their own. If the book reached its final form later, it would also have spoken to people facing defeat and exile.', 'The stories cover the generations between Joshua''s conquest and the rise of King Saul. Most scholars place them around 1200 to 1050 BC, though a traditional reckoning based on numbers given in the Bible starts the period earlier, in the 1300s BC. Israel was a loose group of twelve tribes with no capital, no king and no shared army. Canaanite city-states still held strong positions, and neighbouring peoples such as the Moabites, Midianites and Ammonites raided Israelite land. Around this time the Philistines settled on the southern coast and became Israel''s most dangerous rivals.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (8, 'The book of Ruth does not name its author. Jewish tradition, recorded in the Talmud, credits the prophet Samuel, the last of Israel''s judges, who anointed its first kings, Saul and David. Many scholars instead think an unnamed Israelite storyteller wrote it later, either during the time of the kings or after the Jewish exile in Babylon.', 'The first hearers were Israelites, and the book ends by tracing the family line of David, Israel''s great king, back to Ruth. If Samuel wrote it, as Jewish tradition holds, David''s reign still lay ahead for those first hearers. If it was written during the time of the kings, they knew David as their great king and were living under rulers from his family. If it was written after the exile, they were a small community rebuilding life in Judah and wrestling with questions about marriage to foreigners.', 'The story is set in the days of the judges, roughly 1200–1050 BC, before Israel had a king, when the tribes were loosely bound together and times were often unsettled. Life in a small town like Bethlehem revolved around farming, family land and the harvest seasons, and relatives were expected to look after kin who fell on hard times. Moab, Ruth''s homeland, lay east of the Dead Sea and was a long-standing neighbour of Israel and sometimes its enemy. The book itself was written down later, perhaps near the end of this period, or anywhere up to several centuries afterwards, depending on who wrote it.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (9, 'The book never names its author. Jewish tradition credits Samuel, the prophet, priest and judge raised at the sanctuary of Shiloh, with the prophets Nathan and Gad finishing it, since Samuel dies partway through. Many scholars think it was compiled later from older records, songs and stories, perhaps as part of a longer history running from Joshua to Kings, shaped around the time of the Babylonian exile in the 500s BC. First and Second Samuel were originally a single book.', 'It was written for the people of Israel, telling how their nation came to have kings and how David''s family came to the throne. If it reached its final form during the exile, its first readers were Judeans living in Babylon after Jerusalem fell in 586 BC, having lost their king, their temple and their land.', 'The events took place roughly between 1100 and 1000 BC, as Israel moved from a loose group of tribes, led by occasional leaders called judges, to a single kingdom under Saul and then David. The Philistines, a seafaring people who had settled Canaan''s southern coast in five strong cities, were pressing inland and, the book says, kept the Israelites from having blacksmiths of their own. The great powers of Egypt and Mesopotamia were weak at the time, leaving room for small nations to rise and fight over the land.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (10, 'The book does not name its author. Jewish tradition credited the prophets Samuel, Nathan and Gad, though Samuel dies before these events begin. Many scholars think it was put together later, perhaps around the time of the exile in Babylon (500s BC), by writers who wove older court records and stories into one long history of Israel. Others hold that much of it was written much earlier, close to David''s own time. 1 and 2 Samuel were originally a single book.', 'It was first written for the people of Israel, especially those who cared about King David and his royal family line. If the book reached its final form around the exile, its first readers had lost their land, temple and king, and were looking back on how their kingdom began.', 'The events cover David''s reign, around 1010 to 970 BC. Israel''s twelve tribes were only loosely joined, and the Philistines, a powerful people on the coast, were a constant threat. David united the tribes, made Jerusalem his capital, and fought neighbouring peoples such as the Moabites, Ammonites, Edomites and Arameans. Kings in the region kept large households with several wives, and fights over who would inherit the throne were common.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (11, 'The writer is not named. Jewish tradition credits the prophet Jeremiah, while many scholars think an unnamed editor or circle of editors shaped the book during the exile in Babylon. Whoever it was drew on older royal records the book itself names, such as the annals of Solomon and the chronicles of the kings of Israel and Judah. First and Second Kings were originally one book, split later for convenience.', 'The first readers were most likely Jews living after the fall of Jerusalem in 586 BC, many of them exiles in Babylon. Their kings, temple and homeland were gone, and they were asking how their nation had come to this.', 'The book covers roughly 970 to 850 BC, from King David''s death and Solomon''s reign to the split of the kingdom and the rival kings of Israel in the north and Judah in the south. Small kingdoms such as Tyre, Aram and Moab traded and fought around them, while Egypt and later Assyria loomed as great powers. The finished work, ending in 2 Kings, dates from after about 560 BC, when Babylon had destroyed Jerusalem and its temple.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (12, 'The writer of 2 Kings is not named; 1 and 2 Kings were originally one book. Jewish tradition credits the prophet Jeremiah. Many scholars instead see it as the work of unnamed editors, possibly priests or scribes, who shaped a larger history running from Deuteronomy through Kings, perhaps with a first edition under King Josiah and a final one during the exile. Either way, the writer drew on older royal records and stories of the prophets, some of which are named in the book.', 'The finished book was first read by Jewish people after the Babylonians destroyed Jerusalem in 586 BC (some date it 587), many of them living as exiles in Babylon and others among the ruins at home. With no king, no temple and no land of their own, they were asking how their nation had come to fall.', 'The book covers roughly 850 to 560 BC, when God''s people were split into two kingdoms: Israel in the north, with its capital at Samaria, and Judah in the south, ruled from Jerusalem by kings descended from David. Great empires pressed in on them. Assyria conquered Israel in 722 BC, and Babylon later destroyed Jerusalem and its temple. The book''s last event, around 561 BC, shows it was completed during the exile.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (13, 'Jewish tradition names Ezra, the priest and scribe who led a group of exiles home from Babylon and taught God''s law in Jerusalem. Many scholars instead think the author was an unnamed writer, or a circle of writers, probably Levites (temple assistants and musicians) connected to the rebuilt temple, often simply called ''the Chronicler.'' Either way, the writer worked from older records, including the books of Samuel and Kings, genealogies and temple lists, retelling Israel''s story with special attention to David and to worship.', 'It was written for Jews living in and around Jerusalem after the exile in Babylon, a small community rebuilding their lives under foreign rule. They had a temple again but no king of David''s line, and many wondered whether they were still connected to the Israel of old and to God''s promises.', 'The book was most likely written in the Persian period, around 450–350 BC, though some scholars place it a little later, early in the Greek era. Judah was then a small province of the vast Persian Empire. The temple had been rebuilt around 516 BC, and leaders like Ezra and Nehemiah had worked to restore the community''s worship, walls and identity. The events the book describes, chiefly David''s reign, lay more than five hundred years in the past.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (14, 'Jewish tradition credits Ezra, a priest and scribe who helped lead the return from exile in Babylon. Many scholars instead think of an unnamed writer, often called ''the Chronicler'', probably a Levite (a member of the tribe that served at the temple) living in Jerusalem. Either way, the writer worked from older records, including the books of Samuel and Kings, and clearly loved the temple, its music and its worship. First and Second Chronicles were originally one book.', 'It was written for Jews who had come back from exile in Babylon to a small, poor homeland around Jerusalem, ruled by foreign powers. They had a rebuilt temple but no king, and they were asking whether God''s promises to David and to their ancestors still held for them.', 'Most scholars date the book to around 400–350 BC, when the Persian Empire ruled the Jewish province called Judah. Jerusalem had been destroyed by the Babylonians in 586 BC, and the Persian king Cyrus later let exiles return and rebuild the temple. Life centred on the temple and its priests rather than a royal court. The book looks back over about four hundred years of Judah''s kings, from Solomon to the exile.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (15, 'Jewish tradition holds that Ezra himself wrote this book. He was a priest descended from Aaron, Israel''s first high priest, and a scribe, meaning an expert who copied, studied and taught the law of Moses. Several chapters are told in his own voice, and the book also includes official Persian letters and lists of families. Many scholars think a later editor, possibly the same person or circle behind the books of Chronicles, put the book together, perhaps around 400 BC, using Ezra''s own memoirs and these documents. Ezra and Nehemiah were originally counted as a single book.', 'The first readers were Jews living in and around Jerusalem after the exile, when many of their families had been taken captive to Babylon. They were a small, often poor community inside the vast Persian Empire. They were rebuilding their temple, their city and their life as a people, and often faced suspicious neighbours and distant officials.', 'In 539 BC the Persian king Cyrus conquered Babylon, and he soon allowed exiled peoples, including the Jews, to return home. Judah became a small district in a Persian province, governed under a king who lived far away. The book covers eighty years or more: the first return and the rebuilding of the temple, completed around 515 BC, and then, after a long gap (nearly sixty years on the traditional dating), Ezra''s own journey to Jerusalem.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (16, 'Much of this book is the personal memoir of Nehemiah, a Jewish man living in exile who served as cupbearer to the Persian king Artaxerxes I. A cupbearer tasted and served the king''s wine, a trusted post close to the throne. The king later appointed Nehemiah governor of Judah. Jewish tradition treats Ezra and Nehemiah as one book, possibly put together by Ezra, while many scholars think a later editor combined Nehemiah''s memoir with other records and lists.', 'It was first read by the small Jewish community in and around Jerusalem, descendants of exiles who had come back from Babylon. Traditionally the book is placed soon after Nehemiah''s time, though many scholars think it reached its final form somewhat later, around 400 BC or after. Its first readers were poor, few in number and surrounded by unfriendly neighbours, and their city''s walls had been rebuilt in Nehemiah''s day.', 'Judah was a small province of the vast Persian Empire. The Babylonians had destroyed Jerusalem around 586 BC, and from around 538 BC Persian kings allowed Jewish exiles to return. The temple was rebuilt by around 516 BC, but the city walls remained broken. Nehemiah''s work begins around 445 BC, in the reign of Artaxerxes I.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (17, 'The writer of Esther is not named. Jewish tradition credits the book to the ''Men of the Great Assembly,'' a council of Jewish leaders after the exile, and some have suggested Mordecai himself. Many scholars think an unknown Jewish author wrote it some decades or more after the events, in the later Persian period or a little after. Whoever it was knew the Persian royal court well and wrote as a skilled storyteller.', 'The first readers were Jews scattered far from Jerusalem, in lands ruled by Persia or, if the book was written a little later, by the Greek kings who came after. They were a small minority living under foreign rulers, often exposed to suspicion and danger. The book also gave them the background to the yearly feast of Purim, which they kept together as a community.', 'The story takes place in Susa, one of the Persian Empire''s royal capitals, during the reign of Ahasuerus, usually identified with Xerxes I (around 486–465 BC). Persia then ruled a vast territory stretching from India to Cush, the land south of Egypt (some Bibles translate it ''Ethiopia''). Decades earlier King Cyrus had allowed exiled Jews to return home, but many had stayed where they were, building lives in Persian cities. Many readers take the story as straightforward history; some scholars see it as a tale set in a real Persian court rather than a record of events, partly because no record outside the Bible mentions Esther and Greek historians give Xerxes a queen with a different name.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (18, 'No one knows who wrote Job, and the book never names its author. Jewish tradition credited it to Moses, and some have suggested Solomon or Job himself, but many scholars think it was written by an unnamed Israelite poet steeped in Israel''s ''wisdom'' tradition, the kind of teachers who reflected on how life works. Whoever wrote it was a master of Hebrew poetry with a remarkably rich vocabulary, and framed the long poetic heart of the book with a short prose story at the beginning and end.', 'Its first readers were Israelites who knew the wisdom teaching of their day, which taught that the good tend to prosper and the wicked to suffer. If the book comes from the time of exile or soon after, as many think, it spoke to a people who had lost their land, temple and king and were wrestling with why such suffering had come upon them.', 'The story is set in an older world much like the days of Abraham. Wealth is counted in flocks, herds and servants, Job offers sacrifices himself as head of his family, and there is no mention of Israel, its law or its temple. Job lives in the land of Uz, probably in Edom or northern Arabia, east of Israel. When the book was actually written is uncertain. The Jewish tradition that Moses wrote it places it very early; other proposals range from Solomon''s time, around 950 BC, to after the exile, around 500–400 BC, with many scholars favouring the later period.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (19, 'Psalms is a collection of 150 songs and prayers by many writers over several centuries. The headings credit about half to King David, a shepherd who became Israel''s warrior-king and a skilled musician. Others are credited to Asaph and the ''sons of Korah'' (families of Levites who led temple music), to King Solomon, and one to Moses, while many carry no name. Many scholars think the headings were added later, and they debate whether the headings always mean authorship, since ''of David'' can also mean ''for'' or ''in the style of'' David.', 'These songs were first sung by the people of Israel in worship, especially at the temple in Jerusalem, where Levite choirs led with harps, lyres, cymbals and trumpets. Over time the collection became Israel''s hymnbook and prayer book, used by pilgrims at festivals, by Jews returning from exile, and later in synagogues.', 'The psalms span many centuries. Tradition traces the oldest to Moses, though many scholars date the earliest to around King David''s time, about 1000 BC, when Jerusalem became the capital and Solomon then built the temple. They cover the divided kingdoms, threats from Assyria and Babylon, Jerusalem''s destruction in 586 BC and the exile in Babylon. The collection likely reached its final form after the return under Persian rule, when the temple was rebuilt around 516 BC.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (20, 'Proverbs is traditionally linked to Solomon, son of David, king of Israel around 950 BC, remembered for his great wisdom and for speaking thousands of proverbs. The book itself names other contributors too: unnamed ''wise'' teachers, Agur, King Lemuel, and the scribes of King Hezekiah who copied out Solomon''s sayings. Many scholars think the book grew over several centuries, gathered by royal scribes and teachers, with its final form perhaps after the Jews returned from exile in Babylon.', 'Much of the book speaks to a ''son,'' likely young men being trained for adult life, whether by parents at home or by teachers preparing future officials at the royal court. Over time it became shared teaching for ordinary Israelite households, guiding everyday choices in work, family, money and speech.', 'The Bible remembers Solomon''s reign as a rare time of peace and wealth for Israel, with busy foreign trade, a splendid court and the building of the first Temple in Jerusalem, though historians and archaeologists debate how large and grand his kingdom really was. Neighbouring lands such as Egypt and Mesopotamia had their own long traditions of wise sayings, often used to train young officials, and Israel''s teachers shared in this wider world. Later collecting happened under King Hezekiah around 700 BC, when the small kingdom of Judah lived under the shadow of the mighty Assyrian empire.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (21, 'The book names its writer as ''the Teacher'' (in Hebrew, Qoheleth, a word linked to gathering, probably someone who gathers people to teach them), a son of David and king in Jerusalem. Jewish and Christian tradition has long identified him as King Solomon, famed for his wisdom and wealth. Many scholars think it was written later by a wise teacher who spoke in Solomon''s voice, partly because its Hebrew resembles later writings. Either way, the writer belonged to Israel''s tradition of wise men who taught through sayings and reflections on life.', 'It was written for Israelites who wanted to think carefully about work, wealth, pleasure, injustice and death. If written by Solomon, its first readers lived in Israel''s prosperous golden age. If written later, they were Jews in the small province of Judah, living under foreign rulers and dealing with taxes, officials and trade.', 'On the traditional view, the setting is Solomon''s reign, around 970–930 BC, a time of peace, great building projects, international trade and royal splendour in Jerusalem. On the later view, it comes from around 450–250 BC, when Judah was ruled by the Persian Empire and then by Greek kings after Alexander the Great. Throughout this whole period, nations across the Middle East had their own traditions of wisdom writing about how to live well.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (22, 'The opening line links the book to Solomon, king of Israel around 970–930 BC, the son of King David, remembered for his wisdom and said to have written over a thousand songs. The Hebrew wording can mean a song by Solomon, for him, or about him, so readers have long weighed how directly he wrote it. Traditionally Solomon is the author. Many scholars think the poems were written or gathered later by unnamed poets, perhaps over a long period, partly because of features in the Hebrew language.', 'No one knows exactly who first heard these poems. They may have been enjoyed at a royal court, among ordinary people, or both. Whoever they were, they knew vineyards, flocks, gardens and wild hill country, the world the poems are full of. The songs may have been sung at weddings and celebrations, and later Jewish communities read the whole book aloud each year during Passover, the spring festival remembering the escape from Egypt.', 'If written in Solomon''s day, it comes from Israel''s golden age: peace, wealth, the building of the first temple in Jerusalem, and trade with Egypt, Phoenicia and Arabia. Love poetry was well known across the ancient Middle East, and Egyptian love songs from around 1300–1100 BC use similar images. The poems name real places across the land, from Jerusalem and En-gedi to Lebanon, Gilead and Carmel.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (23, 'Isaiah son of Amoz was a prophet in Jerusalem, active for about forty years, from around 740 to 700 BC, under Kings Uzziah, Jotham, Ahaz and Hezekiah. He moved in royal circles and spoke directly to kings. He was married, and he gave his sons names that carried messages. Tradition credits him with the whole book; many scholars think chapters 40–66 were added by later prophets in his tradition, writing during and after the exile in Babylon.', 'The first hearers were the people of Jerusalem and Judah, including their kings and leaders. They lived under constant threat from Assyria and were tempted to trust in foreign alliances. Many scholars think chapters 40–66 were written for a later situation: Jews exiled in Babylon after Jerusalem''s destruction, and then those returning to rebuild a ruined city. Traditionally, these chapters are read as Isaiah''s own words, looking ahead to that time.', 'Isaiah lived as Assyria, a ruthless empire based in what is now northern Iraq, pushed west. In 722 BC Assyria destroyed the northern kingdom of Israel, and in 701 BC its king Sennacherib overran Judah and besieged Jerusalem. Judah''s kings wavered between submitting to Assyria and seeking help from Egypt. The later chapters look to Babylon''s destruction of Jerusalem in 587 BC, the exile that followed, and the rise of Cyrus of Persia, who let the exiles return from 538 BC.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (24, 'Jeremiah was the son of Hilkiah, from a family of priests in Anathoth, a small village a few miles north of Jerusalem. He was called as a prophet, someone who spoke God''s messages to the people, while still young, and kept speaking for about forty years, often at great personal cost. His call is traditionally dated to around 627 BC, though some scholars think that date marks his birth and that his public work began nearer 609 BC. His friend and scribe Baruch wrote down many of his words. Traditionally Jeremiah is seen as the book''s source; many scholars think his followers later collected and arranged it, noting that the ancient Greek version is shorter and ordered differently.', 'Jeremiah spoke first to the kings, priests and ordinary people of Judah and its capital, Jerusalem, a small kingdom caught between powerful empires. Many of them refused to listen, and some tried to silence him. Later parts address those deported to Babylon and the refugees who fled to Egypt after Jerusalem fell.', 'Jeremiah lived through the collapse of the Assyrian Empire and the rise of Babylon. Good King Josiah led a sweeping religious reform around 622 BC but was killed in battle by Egypt''s Pharaoh Necho in 609 BC. After Babylon defeated Egypt at Carchemish in 605 BC, Judah became its subject, and repeated rebellions led to a first deportation of Jerusalem''s leaders in 597 BC. In 587 or 586 BC the Babylonians destroyed Jerusalem and its temple and carried many more into exile.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (25, 'The book itself names no author. Jewish and Christian tradition has long credited it to Jeremiah, a prophet who lived through the fall of Jerusalem and had warned of it for years. Many scholars instead think it came from one or more unnamed poets in Judah who saw the disaster firsthand. Either way, the writer was a skilled poet, shaping most chapters as alphabet poems in which each verse or group of verses begins with the next letter of the Hebrew alphabet.', 'The first hearers were survivors of Jerusalem''s destruction, mostly people left behind among the ruins of Judah, and perhaps also those carried off to Babylon. Their city lay burned, their temple was gone, and many had lost family to war, famine and exile. The poems were likely recited at gatherings where people mourned the ruined temple, and Jewish communities still read them each year on a fast day remembering its destruction.', 'Around 588 BC the armies of Nebuchadnezzar, king of the powerful Babylonian empire, surrounded Jerusalem after Judah rebelled. The siege brought terrible famine, and in 587 or 586 BC the walls were broken, the temple Solomon had built was burned, and King Zedekiah was captured. Many leading citizens were marched into exile in Babylon, while neighbouring peoples looked on and some joined in the plunder.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (26, 'The book is traditionally credited to Ezekiel, the son of Buzi, a priest from Jerusalem trained to serve in the temple. He was taken to Babylon as a captive in 597 BC, along with King Jehoiachin and many of Judah''s leading families. He lived among the exiles in a settlement called Tel Abib (the modern Israeli city of Tel Aviv later took its name from it), beside the Kebar canal. There, around 593 BC, he was called to be a prophet; most scholars think much of the book comes from him, though some believe followers later arranged and expanded it.', 'The first hearers were Judeans living as exiles (people forced from their homeland) in Babylon, far from Jerusalem and its temple. Many hoped for a quick return home and believed Jerusalem could never fall. After the city was destroyed in 586 BC, they were left grieving the loss of their city, their king and their temple.', 'The Babylonian empire under King Nebuchadnezzar dominated the region, and Judah was a subject kingdom paying tribute. After a first deportation in 597 BC, Babylon placed Zedekiah on Judah''s throne, but he rebelled, hoping Egypt would help. Babylon besieged Jerusalem from about 588 BC and destroyed the city and Solomon''s temple in 586 BC. Ezekiel''s dated messages span roughly 593 to 571 BC.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (27, 'Traditionally the book is credited to Daniel, a young Jew from a noble or royal family who was taken from Jerusalem to Babylon as a teenager around 605 BC. He was trained to serve in the Babylonian court and rose to become a senior royal official and a trusted interpreter of dreams under both Babylonian and Persian kings. Chapters 1–6 tell stories about him in the third person, while chapters 7–12 record his visions in his own voice. Many modern scholars think the book reached its final form around 165 BC, when an unnamed faithful Jewish writer in Judea gathered older stories about Daniel and added the visions.', 'On the traditional view, the first readers were Jews living in exile in Babylon and afterwards, serving under powerful foreign rulers far from home. On the later-date view, the book was written for Jews in Judea around 167–164 BC, when the Greek-Syrian king Antiochus IV outlawed their faith on pain of death. In both pictures, the readers were God''s people living under powerful foreign empires.', 'The stories span from around 605 BC, when the Babylonian king Nebuchadnezzar first took captives from Judah, through his destruction of Jerusalem and its Temple in 586 BC, when many more of its people were carried off to Babylon, to the early years of Persian rule. In 539 BC Babylon fell to Cyrus of Persia, who soon allowed exiles to go home and rebuild. If the book was finished in the 160s BC, its writer lived under Greek rulers descended from Alexander the Great''s generals, during Antiochus IV''s persecution and the Jewish uprising led by the Maccabee family. In Jewish Bibles, Daniel sits among the Writings rather than the Prophets.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (28, 'The book is named after Hosea son of Beeri, a prophet: someone believed to speak messages from God to his people. He is the only prophet from the northern kingdom of Israel whose messages to his own people fill a book of the Bible. Little is known of his daily life beyond his troubled marriage to a woman named Gomer, which the book describes. Most take this as a real marriage, though some, both in church tradition and among scholars, have read it as a vision or symbolic story. Most agree the messages go back to Hosea himself, though many scholars think they were later collected and edited in the southern kingdom of Judah, with a few lines added for readers there.', 'Hosea first spoke to the people of the northern kingdom of Israel, which he often calls ''Ephraim'' after its leading tribe, and to its leaders in the capital, Samaria. Many mixed worship of Israel''s God with worship of Baal, a Canaanite god believed to bring rain and good harvests, and the kingdom''s official shrines held golden calf statues. After the north fell, the collected messages were treasured by readers in Judah to the south.', 'Hosea was active from roughly 750 to 720 BC. He began during the long, prosperous reign of King Jeroboam II, but after Jeroboam''s death the northern kingdom fell into chaos, with six kings in about 30 years, four of them murdered. The mighty Assyrian empire, based in what is now Iraq, was expanding westward, and Israel''s rulers swung between paying it off and seeking help from Egypt. In 722 BC Assyria captured Samaria and carried many Israelites into exile, ending the northern kingdom.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (29, 'The book names its author as Joel son of Pethuel, and nothing more is known about him or his family. He was a prophet, someone who spoke messages from God to the people. He knew Jerusalem, its temple and its priests well, which suggests he lived in or near the city, and some think he may have had close links with temple worship.', 'Joel spoke to the people of Judah and Jerusalem, including the priests who led worship at the temple and the elders who led the community. They had just been struck by a devastating swarm of locusts and a drought that stripped the fields, vineyards and orchards bare. Food was scarce, and even the grain and wine used in daily temple offerings had run out.', 'The date of Joel is unclear because the book names no king. Traditionally it has been placed early, around 800 BC, perhaps while the boy-king Joash ruled Judah under the guidance of priests. Many scholars today date it later, around 500–350 BC, after the Jews returned from exile in Babylon and rebuilt the temple, when Judah was a small province under Persian rule. Either way, Judah was a small land whose people lived mostly by farming and whose survival depended on rain and harvest, and locust swarms were a dreaded and familiar disaster.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (30, 'The book is named after Amos, a man from Tekoa, a small village in the hill country of Judah, a few miles south of Bethlehem. He was not a professional prophet or a priest; he worked as a herdsman of sheep and also tended sycamore-fig trees. He travelled north to deliver his messages in the neighbouring kingdom of Israel. Most scholars accept that the core of the book comes from Amos himself, though many think some passages were added or arranged later by those who preserved his words.', 'His first hearers were the people of the northern kingdom of Israel, especially the wealthy in the capital, Samaria, and worshippers at the royal shrine of Bethel. They were living through a time of peace and prosperity, busy with festivals and offerings, while a sharp gap had opened between rich landowners and the poor.', 'Amos spoke around 760–750 BC, when Uzziah ruled Judah in the south and Jeroboam II ruled Israel in the north. With the great empire of Assyria (in what is now Iraq) temporarily weakened, Jeroboam had won back territory and trade flourished, bringing fine houses and luxury goods to the upper classes. Within a few decades Assyria grew strong again, and in 722 BC it destroyed Samaria and ended the northern kingdom.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (31, 'The book is credited to Obadiah, whose name means ''servant of the Lord''. He was a prophet, someone who brought God''s messages to the people, but nothing else is known about his family, hometown or life. Ancient Jewish tradition identified him with the Obadiah who served in King Ahab''s household, though most scholars today think this is unlikely.', 'The message is addressed to Edom, a neighbouring nation descended from Esau, Jacob''s brother. Its real first hearers, however, were the people of Judah, stung by the way the Edomites had looked on and joined in when Jerusalem was attacked and plundered, at a time whose exact date is still debated.', 'Most scholars connect the book to the Babylonian destruction of Jerusalem in 586 BC, when Edom sided with the invaders and took advantage of Judah''s collapse. Some instead date it earlier, to around 845 BC, when Philistines and Arabs raided Jerusalem during King Jehoram''s reign. Either way, Israel and Edom had a long history of bitter rivalry stretching back centuries, despite their shared ancestry.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (32, 'The book does not name its writer. Traditionally it is linked to Jonah son of Amittai himself, a prophet from the village of Gath-hepher in Galilee who served in the northern kingdom of Israel under King Jeroboam II, around 780–750 BC. Many scholars think an unknown Israelite writer told Jonah''s story much later, perhaps after the Jews returned from exile in Babylon, around 500–400 BC, partly because the story is told about Jonah rather than by him, and because of features of its Hebrew.', 'On the traditional view, the first hearers were Israelites in the northern kingdom, who knew Assyria as a powerful and brutal empire to the north-east, whose armies had troubled the region before and would soon return. If the book was written later, its readers were Jews rebuilding their life in and around Jerusalem after the exile, under Persian rule and living alongside many other peoples.', 'In Jonah''s day the northern kingdom of Israel was enjoying a rare stretch of peace and prosperity under Jeroboam II, regaining land it had lost. To the east lay Assyria, a great empire known for its brutal warfare, and Nineveh, on the River Tigris near modern Mosul in Iraq, was one of its greatest cities. A few decades later, in 722 BC, Assyria would conquer Israel and carry many of its people away.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (33, 'The book is linked to Micah of Moresheth, a small town in the low hill country of Judah about 25 miles southwest of Jerusalem. He was a prophet, someone who brought messages from God to the people. He came from the countryside, not the royal court, and spoke up for small farmers. Traditionally Micah wrote the whole book; many scholars think his words were later gathered and expanded by followers, especially the hopeful passages in chapters 4–7.', 'Micah''s messages were first spoken to the people of Judah and its capital Jerusalem, and also to the northern kingdom of Israel and its capital Samaria. He spoke especially to rulers, judges, priests and prophets, and to wealthy landowners who were taking land and homes from poorer families.', 'Micah prophesied around 740–700 BC, during the reigns of Judah''s kings Jotham, Ahaz and Hezekiah, at the same time as the prophet Isaiah. The Assyrian Empire, based in what is now northern Iraq, was conquering the region. Assyria destroyed Samaria in 722 BC, and in 701 BC its king Sennacherib invaded Judah, capturing many towns near Micah''s home.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (34, 'The book is credited to Nahum, a prophet described only as coming from Elkosh, a village whose location is now unknown. Some suggest a site in Galilee or southern Judah, but nobody can be sure. A prophet was someone believed to carry messages from God to the people. Beyond his name and hometown, nothing else is known about his life or family.', 'Nahum''s words were first heard by the people of Judah, the small southern kingdom centred on Jerusalem. For generations they had lived under the shadow of Assyria, paying it heavy tribute and fearing its armies. Assyria had already wiped out the northern kingdom of Israel and once besieged Jerusalem itself.', 'Assyria, ruled from its capital Nineveh, was the greatest and most feared empire of its day, known for brutal punishment of conquered peoples. The book mentions the Assyrian sack of the Egyptian city of Thebes in about 663 BC, and Nineveh itself fell in 612 BC, so most date Nahum between these two events. Some place him soon after Thebes fell, while others place him closer to Nineveh''s collapse. Over those decades Assyria weakened, Babylon and the Medes rose against it, and in Judah the long rule of King Manasseh gave way to the reforms of King Josiah.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (35, 'The book is credited to Habakkuk, who is simply called ''the prophet'', someone who received and passed on messages from God. Almost nothing is known about his family, hometown or life. Because the final chapter is a prayer set to music with instructions for a choir leader, many think he may have served among the prophets connected with worship at the temple in Jerusalem. Most scholars accept him as the source of the book, though some think the closing prayer was first a separate psalm that was later joined to it.', 'His first hearers were the people of Judah, the small southern kingdom centred on Jerusalem, at a time when a new and frightening empire was rising in the east. Most readers take the book''s opening complaint to describe life in Judah itself: violence, unfair courts and powerful people exploiting the weak. Some scholars think it instead describes the cruelty of a foreign oppressor such as Assyria or Babylon.', 'Habakkuk is usually dated to the years around 600 BC, probably between about 609 and 597 BC. The old Assyrian empire had collapsed, its capital Nineveh falling in 612 BC, and Babylon was rising quickly, defeating Egypt at the battle of Carchemish in 605 BC. In Judah, good King Josiah had been killed in 609 BC, and his son Jehoiakim''s rule is remembered as harsh and unjust. Within a few years Babylon would invade Judah, carrying off captives in 597 BC and destroying Jerusalem in 586 BC.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (36, 'Zephaniah was a prophet, someone who spoke messages he believed came from God, and he seems to have lived in or near Jerusalem. The book''s opening line traces his family back four generations to a man named Hezekiah, possibly the well-known King Hezekiah, which would make Zephaniah a distant relative of the royal family, though this is not certain. Beyond this family line, almost nothing is known about his life.', 'His first hearers were the people of Judah, the small southern kingdom of the Israelites, and especially the residents of its capital, Jerusalem. Many had drifted into worshipping other gods alongside the God of Israel, and the leaders and wealthy classes were comfortable and confident.', 'The book places Zephaniah in the reign of King Josiah, who ruled Judah from about 640 to 609 BC, and many think he spoke before Josiah''s major religious reforms, which reached their height around 622 BC. Before Josiah, King Manasseh''s long reign had filled Judah with the worship of foreign gods. The great empire of Assyria, which had dominated the region for a century, was weakening, and Babylon was rising; Assyria''s capital, Nineveh, fell in 612 BC.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (37, 'Haggai was a prophet, someone called to speak God''s messages to the people, who lived in Jerusalem after the Jews returned from exile in Babylon. We know almost nothing about his family or background; outside this book he appears only briefly in the book of Ezra, alongside the prophet Zechariah. Traditionally Haggai is seen as the author of his own messages, though because the book speaks of him in the third person, many scholars think a follower or editor gathered and arranged his words soon after he spoke them.', 'Haggai first spoke to the small community of Jews who had come back to Jerusalem and the surrounding land of Judah, along with their leaders, Zerubbabel the governor and Joshua the high priest (the chief priest). About sixteen years earlier they had begun rebuilding God''s temple, but the work had stalled amid opposition from neighbours, poor harvests and the struggle to rebuild their own homes and lives.', 'Haggai''s messages are dated to 520 BC, in the second year of Darius I, king of the vast Persian Empire, who had seized the throne after a period of upheaval and revolts. Persia had conquered Babylon in 539 BC, and its king Cyrus allowed exiled Jews to return home and rebuild the temple that the Babylonians had destroyed in 586 BC. Judah was now a small, poor province within the Persian Empire, and Jerusalem still lay largely in ruins. The rebuilt temple was finished around 516 BC.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (38, 'Zechariah was a prophet, a man who brought messages he believed came from God. He was the son of Berechiah and grandson of Iddo, and he likely came from a priestly family that had returned from exile in Babylon. He worked alongside the prophet Haggai, beginning around 520 BC. Chapters 1–8 are widely accepted as his, but many scholars think chapters 9–14 come from a later writer or writers, while others hold that Zechariah wrote the whole book.', 'The first hearers were Jews who had come back to Jerusalem and Judah after decades of exile in Babylon. They lived in a small, poor community among ruins, and their work rebuilding God''s temple had stalled for years amid hardship, opposition and discouragement.', 'The Persian Empire ruled the region after defeating Babylon, and in 538 BC the Persian king Cyrus allowed exiled Jews to return home. Foundations for a new temple were laid, but work stalled until 520 BC. King Darius I seized the throne in 522 BC and spent his first years putting down revolts across the empire. Under the encouragement of Haggai and Zechariah the temple was finally completed around 516 BC.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (39, 'Almost nothing is known about the writer beyond the name Malachi, which means ''my messenger'' in Hebrew. Tradition treats it as the personal name of a prophet, someone called to speak God''s word to the people. Many scholars think it may instead be a title drawn from a phrase later in the book, leaving the prophet unnamed. Either way, he was clearly someone who knew the temple, its priests and its worship well.', 'The book was first addressed to the Jewish community in and around Jerusalem, especially the priests who served at the rebuilt temple. They were a small, poor community under foreign rule, and many had grown tired and doubtful as the hopes stirred by their return from exile seemed to go unfulfilled.', 'The book is usually dated to around 460 to 430 BC, when Judah was a minor province of the vast Persian Empire, ruled by a Persian-appointed governor. Jewish exiles had been allowed home from Babylon about a century earlier, and the temple had been rebuilt by around 516 BC. Worship had resumed, but enthusiasm had faded. The book is often placed close to the time of Ezra and Nehemiah, leaders who faced many of the same problems.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (40, 'Christians have traditionally credited this gospel to Matthew, also called Levi, a tax collector who left his booth to become one of Jesus'' twelve apostles. Tax collectors gathered tolls for the ruling powers, often took extra for themselves, and were widely despised by fellow Jews. Many modern scholars think the book was written by an unnamed Jewish Christian teacher who drew on Mark''s gospel and a collection of Jesus'' sayings, with Matthew''s name attached early on. Either way, the writer knew the Jewish Scriptures and customs closely.', 'It was written mainly for Jewish followers of Jesus, perhaps in Syria around the city of Antioch or in Galilee, along with non-Jews joining their churches. They were working out how faith in Jesus fitted with their Jewish heritage, often amid painful arguments with local synagogue leaders.', 'Jesus lived under Roman rule, with local kings from Herod''s family and Roman governors such as Pontius Pilate in charge. The gospel was most likely written around AD 70–90, though some date it to the 60s. In AD 66 the Jews revolted against Rome, and in AD 70 Roman armies destroyed Jerusalem and its temple. Afterwards, Jewish life regrouped largely around the synagogue and the teaching of the Pharisees.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (41, 'The Gospel itself never names its writer, but from the early second century Christians credited it to Mark, traditionally identified with John Mark, a Jewish believer from Jerusalem whose mother''s house was a meeting place for the first church. He travelled with his cousin Barnabas and with Paul, and later worked alongside Peter, and an early church leader named Papias said Mark wrote down Peter''s memories of Jesus. Many scholars accept this tradition, while others think the author was an unknown Christian teacher. Most agree it was probably the first of the four Gospels to be written.', 'Early tradition says Mark wrote for Christians in Rome, many of them not Jewish, which fits the way he explains Jewish customs and translates Aramaic words, the everyday language Jesus spoke. These believers may have been living through, or just after, the fierce persecution under Emperor Nero around AD 64, when Peter is said to have died. Some scholars instead suggest a church in Syria or Galilee.', 'Mark was probably written around AD 65–70, a frightening time when Jews in Judea rose up against Rome and the Roman army crushed the revolt, destroying Jerusalem''s temple in AD 70. The events it describes took place about forty years earlier, around AD 30, in Galilee and Judea under Roman rule. Herod Antipas, a son of Herod the Great, governed Galilee, while the Roman governor Pontius Pilate ruled Judea.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (42, 'From early times the church has named Luke as the writer: a doctor and travelling companion of the apostle Paul, who mentions him in his letters. He was probably a Gentile (not Jewish), well educated and writing in polished Greek, and he also wrote the book of Acts as a second volume. He was not an eyewitness of Jesus'' life but gathered accounts from those who were. Many accept this tradition, though others think the author was an unknown later Christian, perhaps connected with Paul''s circle.', 'Luke addresses his work to Theophilus, probably a well-placed patron or official, possibly a new believer. Behind him stood a wider circle of mostly Gentile Christians spread across the Roman Empire, many of whom had never seen Judea. They wanted a careful, orderly account of the faith they had joined.', 'Jesus lived under Roman rule, when Judea and Galilee were governed by Roman officials and local kings from Herod''s family. Luke wrote decades later, as the church was spreading from Jewish communities into Greek-speaking cities across the empire. Traditionally the book is dated to the early 60s AD, though many scholars place it around AD 80–90, after the Romans destroyed Jerusalem in AD 70.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (43, 'Christians have traditionally held that this Gospel was written by John, son of Zebedee, a fisherman from Galilee who became one of Jesus'' twelve closest followers. The book itself points to an unnamed ''disciple whom Jesus loved'' as its eyewitness source. Many scholars think it was written down by a follower or a circle of followers of that disciple, or by another early Christian leader named John, drawing on his testimony.', 'Early tradition places its first readers around Ephesus, a large city in what is now western Turkey, though this is not certain. They seem to have been a mix of Jewish and non-Jewish believers. The writer explains Jewish customs and translates Hebrew and Aramaic words, so not every reader knew them. Some may have been pushed out of their local synagogues for following Jesus.', 'The events take place around AD 30, when Judea was under Roman rule through the governor Pontius Pilate and Galilee was ruled by Herod Antipas. The Gospel was most likely written around AD 90, a generation later, though some suggest an earlier date. By then the Romans had destroyed Jerusalem and its temple in AD 70. Jewish communities were rebuilding without the temple, and tensions between synagogues and followers of Jesus were growing.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (44, 'Acts is the second volume of a two-part work; the same writer produced the Gospel of Luke, and both are addressed to a man named Theophilus. Church tradition from the second century names the author as Luke, a doctor and travelling companion of the apostle Paul, probably a non-Jew with a good Greek education. Some passages switch from ''they'' to ''we'', which many take as the writer joining Paul''s journeys. Many scholars accept Luke, while others think an unnamed later Christian wrote it, possibly drawing on a travel diary.', 'The book is formally addressed to Theophilus, possibly a well-placed patron or official, or simply a respected believer who wanted a reliable account. Beyond him, it was meant for the young churches scattered around the Mediterranean, a mix of Jewish and non-Jewish believers working out who they were and how they related to Judaism and to Rome.', 'The events run from about AD 30 to around AD 62, under Roman emperors from Tiberius to Nero, when good roads and shipping lanes linked cities across the empire. Jewish communities with synagogues were found in almost every major city, and Judea was ruled by Roman governors and members of the Herod family amid rising unrest. The book was most likely written somewhere between the early 60s and the 80s AD, though some scholars place it later.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (45, 'The apostle Paul, one of the few New Testament authors whose authorship almost all scholars accept. He was a Jew from Tarsus, in what is now Turkey, trained as a Pharisee (a member of a strict Jewish group devoted to keeping God''s law) and a tentmaker by trade. Once a fierce opponent of Jesus'' followers, he became an apostle, a messenger sent to carry the news about Jesus, especially to Gentiles (non-Jewish people). He dictated this letter to a helper named Tertius, who wrote it down.', 'Christians in Rome, the capital of the empire, meeting in small groups in people''s homes, a mix of Jewish believers and a growing number of Gentile believers. Paul had not founded this church and had never visited it. Around AD 49 the emperor Claudius expelled Jews from Rome, and many returned only after his death in AD 54, which may have left Jewish and Gentile believers adjusting to one another.', 'Paul probably wrote around AD 56–58, most likely from the Greek city of Corinth near the end of his travels around the eastern Mediterranean. The young emperor Nero had come to power, and his early years were seen as calm and well governed, with Roman roads and peace making travel easier. Paul was collecting money from Gentile churches to carry to poor believers in Jerusalem, and he hoped afterwards to visit Rome on his way to preach in Spain.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (46, 'The letter is from Paul, with a co-worker named Sosthenes joined in the greeting. Paul was a Jew from Tarsus, in what is now southern Turkey, trained as a Pharisee (a strict teacher of Jewish law). He once persecuted Christians, but after encountering the risen Jesus he became a travelling missionary, an apostle who started churches across the Roman world. He earned his living as a tentmaker, and almost all scholars agree he wrote this letter.', 'The first readers were the believers in Corinth, a church Paul had started around AD 50–52 during a stay of about eighteen months. Most were non-Jews, along with some Jews, and they met in one another''s homes. A few years after Paul left, he heard reports of quarrels and troubles among them, and they had also written to him with questions.', 'Paul wrote around AD 53–55, most likely from the city of Ephesus, in what is now western Turkey. Corinth was a busy Roman colony in southern Greece, rebuilt by Julius Caesar in 44 BC after an earlier destruction. It sat on a narrow strip of land between two seaports, so it was crowded with traders, sailors, slaves and freed slaves, and full of temples to many gods. The Christian movement was only about twenty-five years old.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (47, 'The letter comes from Paul, a Jewish man from Tarsus who had been a Pharisee, a strict teacher of Jewish law, before becoming a follower of Jesus. He worked as a travelling apostle, a missionary who started churches across the Roman world, and supported himself by making tents. He had founded the church in Corinth himself around AD 50–51. Nearly everyone accepts Paul wrote it, though many scholars think the book joins two or more of his letters to Corinth, while others read it as one letter.', 'It was written to the church in Corinth, mostly non-Jewish converts with some Jewish believers, and to Christians across Achaia, the Roman province of southern Greece. Paul''s relationship with them had become strained, with a painful visit, a sorrowful letter, and visiting rival teachers who questioned his authority.', 'Paul probably wrote around AD 55–56 from Macedonia in northern Greece, during the early reign of the emperor Nero. Corinth was a wealthy, busy Roman colony and trading port, rebuilt by Julius Caesar a century earlier, full of temples, merchants and travelling speakers. Paul was also gathering money from his non-Jewish churches to help poor believers in Jerusalem.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (48, 'The letter comes from Paul, and almost all scholars agree he wrote it. He was a Jew from Tarsus, in what is now southern Turkey, trained as a Pharisee (a member of a Jewish group devoted to strictly keeping God''s law), and once fiercely opposed to the followers of Jesus. After meeting the risen Jesus, he became an apostle, a messenger sent to spread the good news, travelling from city to city founding churches and often supporting himself as a tentmaker.', 'It went to several churches in Galatia, a region in the middle of modern Turkey. Scholars disagree on which: traditionally the Celtic Galatian people in the north, though many think it means towns in the south such as Iconium, Lystra and Derbe, which Paul visited on his first journey. These were mostly non-Jewish believers, and new teachers had arrived insisting they be circumcised and keep the Jewish law to belong fully to God''s people.', 'Paul probably wrote around AD 48–55, while Rome ruled the whole Mediterranean world under Emperor Claudius and then, from AD 54, Nero. Some date it very early, before the church leaders met in Jerusalem to settle this question around AD 49; others date it a few years later. It was a time when the young church, which began among Jews, was working out whether non-Jewish believers had to follow Jewish customs.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (49, 'The letter names Paul as its author. He was a Jewish teacher from Tarsus, trained as a Pharisee (a strict group devoted to God''s law), who worked as a tentmaker and became a travelling apostle, or messenger, of Jesus. He writes as a prisoner, traditionally while under house arrest in Rome around AD 60–62. Many scholars note differences in style and vocabulary from his other letters and think a close follower wrote it in his name, perhaps around AD 80–90.', 'The letter is addressed to Christians in Ephesus, a large port city in the Roman province of Asia, in what is now western Turkey, where Paul had spent about three years. Several of the oldest manuscripts leave out the words "in Ephesus," so many think it was meant to circulate among several churches in the region. The readers were mostly non-Jewish believers, worshipping alongside Jewish Christians in small house churches.', 'The Roman Empire ruled the whole Mediterranean world, and if Paul wrote it, Nero was emperor. Ephesus was one of the empire''s greatest cities, famous for its huge temple to the goddess Artemis, one of the wonders of the ancient world, and known for magic and fortune-telling. Early Christians were still working out how Jews and non-Jews, long divided by custom and religious law, could belong to one community.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (50, 'The apostle Paul wrote this letter, with his younger co-worker Timothy named alongside him. Paul was a Jewish teacher from Tarsus, trained as a Pharisee (a group devoted to strict keeping of God''s law), who once persecuted Christians before becoming a follower of Jesus and a travelling missionary who planted churches around the Mediterranean. He wrote while a prisoner awaiting trial. Traditionally this was in Rome around AD 60–62, though some scholars suggest an earlier imprisonment in Ephesus (mid-50s) or Caesarea.', 'The letter went to the church in Philippi, a city in Macedonia (northern Greece), which Paul had founded around AD 49–50; its first members included a businesswoman named Lydia and a local jailer. It was a small, mostly non-Jewish congregation that faced opposition from outsiders and had generously supported Paul, recently sending him a gift by a messenger named Epaphroditus.', 'The Roman Empire ruled the Mediterranean world, with Nero as emperor at the likely time of writing. Philippi was a Roman colony, resettled with retired army veterans after a famous battle there in 42 BC, and its people were proud of their Roman citizenship and customs. Honouring the emperor was part of civic life, and a group that worshipped another Lord could draw suspicion. Prisoners like Paul often relied on friends to bring food and support while they waited, sometimes for years, for their cases to be heard.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (51, 'The letter opens in the name of Paul, a Jewish teacher and former persecutor of Christians who became a travelling missionary planting churches across the Roman world, with his young co-worker Timothy. Paul writes as a prisoner, traditionally in Rome around AD 60–62, though some suggest an earlier imprisonment in Ephesus. Many scholars think a close follower wrote it in Paul''s name after his death, pointing to differences in style and emphasis; others see no reason to doubt Paul himself.', 'It was written to a small church in Colossae, a town in the Lycus River valley in what is now western Turkey, near the larger cities of Laodicea and Hierapolis. Paul had never visited them, and most believers there seem to have been Gentiles (non-Jews) who had only recently turned to Christ from local religions.', 'The Roman Empire ruled the region, with Nero as emperor from AD 54 to 68. Colossae had once been a busy wool-trading town but was being overshadowed by nearby Laodicea, and the valley was home to a mix of Greek, local Phrygian and Jewish communities. A powerful earthquake struck the area around AD 60. Christians were still a tiny, little-understood group meeting in homes.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (52, 'The letter names Paul, along with his travelling companions Silvanus (also called Silas) and Timothy, and it is almost universally accepted as Paul''s own. Paul was a Jew from Tarsus, in what is now southern Turkey, trained as a Pharisee, a strict teacher of Jewish law. He once persecuted followers of Jesus, then became one of the movement''s leading missionaries, earning his living as a tentmaker as he travelled. This is probably one of his earliest surviving letters, and perhaps the oldest writing in the New Testament.', 'It was written to a young church in Thessalonica, a busy port city and the capital of the Roman province of Macedonia in northern Greece. Most believers there seem to have been non-Jews who had recently turned away from worshipping idols. Paul had been forced to leave the city after only a short stay because of opposition, and the new believers were facing pressure and hostility from their neighbours.', 'The letter was probably written around AD 50–51, during the reign of the emperor Claudius, likely from Corinth in southern Greece. Thessalonica sat on the Via Egnatia, a major Roman road, and was full of traders, temples to many gods, and honours paid to the emperor. Small groups meeting in homes to worship Jesus stood out in such a city and could easily draw suspicion.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (53, 'The letter names Paul as its writer, together with his travelling companions Silas (also called Silvanus) and Timothy. Paul was a Jewish teacher from Tarsus, trained as a Pharisee (a strict keeper of Jewish law), who earned his living as a tentmaker and became a travelling missionary after coming to believe in Jesus. Traditionally the letter is accepted as Paul''s own, written soon after 1 Thessalonians, though many scholars think a later follower wrote it in his name.', 'It was written to a young church in Thessalonica, a busy port and the capital of the Roman province of Macedonia in northern Greece. Most members were non-Jews who had turned from local gods, and Paul had been forced to leave the city after only a short stay. The believers faced hostility from their neighbours and were unsettled by questions about the future.', 'If Paul wrote it, the date is around AD 50–51, probably from Corinth in southern Greece, during the reign of the emperor Claudius. Thessalonica sat on the Via Egnatia, a major Roman road linking east and west, and was a self-governing city loyal to Rome that honoured the emperor alongside its traditional gods. Small groups of Jesus-followers met in homes and often drew suspicion for abandoning the city''s religious customs.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (54, 'The letter names Paul as its writer. He was a Jewish scholar trained as a Pharisee (a strict teacher of God''s law), a tentmaker by trade, and a former persecutor of Christians who became a travelling missionary and founder of churches. Traditionally it is dated late in his life, perhaps in the early-to-mid 60s AD after a release from house arrest in Rome; many scholars instead think a later follower wrote it in Paul''s name to pass on his teaching.', 'It is addressed to Timothy, Paul''s younger co-worker and close friend of many years, the son of a Jewish mother and a Greek father, whom Paul had left in Ephesus to guide the church there. The believers met in homes, and some teachers among them were spreading confusing ideas and stirring up arguments, so Timothy needed help organising leadership and church life.', 'Ephesus was a busy port and one of the largest cities in the Roman Empire, in what is now western Turkey, famous for its huge temple to the goddess Artemis. If Paul wrote the letter, Nero was emperor and Christians were a small, often misunderstood group; if it came later, it probably belongs to the late first or early second century AD. Society was built around households that included family members and slaves, and wealth and status mattered greatly in city life.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (55, 'The letter names Paul as its writer, a Jewish teacher trained as a Pharisee (a strict keeper of Jewish law) and a tentmaker by trade, who became a travelling missionary and founded churches across the Roman world. Traditionally it is his last surviving letter, written from prison in Rome. Many scholars instead think a later follower wrote it in Paul''s name, drawing on his memory and teaching, though many others hold to Paul himself.', 'It is addressed to Timothy, Paul''s younger co-worker for many years, the son of a Jewish mother and a Greek father, apparently leading the church at Ephesus at the time. He faced rival teachers stirring up arguments, believers drifting away, and the danger that came with being linked to an imprisoned leader.', 'If written by Paul, it dates to around AD 64–67, under the emperor Nero, who blamed Christians for the great fire of Rome in AD 64 and had many put to death. Christianity was still a small, unofficial movement with no legal protection. Ephesus, a great port city in what is now western Turkey, was famous for its huge temple to the goddess Artemis. Those who think a follower wrote it usually place it some decades later.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (56, 'The letter opens in the name of Paul, a Jewish teacher from Tarsus (in modern Turkey) who was trained in the law, earned a living as a tentmaker, and became a travelling missionary who started churches across the Roman world. It is addressed to Titus, a non-Jewish (Greek) believer who had worked alongside Paul for years. Traditionally Paul wrote it himself; many scholars think a later follower wrote it in his name, together with 1 and 2 Timothy, which are similar letters known as the Pastoral Letters.', 'The letter went first to Titus, whom Paul had left on the island of Crete to organise young churches in its towns and appoint leaders for them. These small groups of believers, meeting in homes, were being unsettled by rival teachers, and Titus was meant to share the letter''s instructions with them.', 'Crete, a large island south of Greece, had been a Roman province since 67 BC, and ancient writers gave its people a reputation for lying and unruliness. If Paul wrote the letter, it likely dates to the early or mid 60s AD, under the emperor Nero, after Paul''s first imprisonment in Rome; those who see it as a later work place it somewhere between the late first century and the early second century. Christian groups were still small, met in private households, and were often viewed with suspicion by their neighbours.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (57, 'The letter comes from the apostle Paul, a Jewish teacher who had once persecuted Christians before becoming one of the best-known travelling missionaries of the early church. Almost all scholars agree that Paul wrote it. He wrote while held as a prisoner, probably under guard and awaiting trial. His younger co-worker Timothy is named alongside him.', 'The letter is addressed mainly to Philemon, a believer of some means, most likely living in Colossae, a small town in what is now western Turkey. It concerns Onesimus, a slave from Philemon''s household who had left him, met Paul, and become a Christian. Most think Onesimus had run away, though some suggest he went to Paul for help in a dispute with his master.', 'This was the Roman Empire of the mid-first century AD, where slavery was an ordinary part of life and a runaway slave could face harsh punishment. Christians were still a small movement with no church buildings, gathering in private homes. Traditionally Paul wrote from house arrest in Rome around AD 60–62, under the emperor Nero. Some scholars suggest an earlier imprisonment in Ephesus, nearer Colossae, in the mid-50s.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (58, 'No one knows for certain who wrote Hebrews, because the writer never gives a name. Early Christians in the East often credited it to the apostle Paul. Many scholars today doubt this because the style and vocabulary differ from his letters, and other suggestions include Paul''s companions Barnabas or Apollos, or the teacher Priscilla. What is clear is that the writer was a skilled teacher and preacher, wrote some of the most polished Greek in the New Testament, knew the Jewish scriptures deeply, and was a friend of Timothy.', 'The first readers were a particular community of Christians the writer knew well. They are traditionally thought to be Jewish believers, though some scholars think the group was mixed. They had once stood firm through public insults, the imprisonment of friends and the seizure of property, but were now weary and tempted to drift away. A closing greeting from believers from Italy leads many to link them with Rome.', 'The letter was written within the Roman Empire, probably in the AD 60s, while the Jerusalem temple and its sacrifices still stood. Some scholars date it later, to the AD 80s, but it was already known when a church leader in Rome drew heavily on it in a letter of his own, usually dated to around AD 96. Christians were increasingly seen as separate from Judaism and therefore lost some of the protection Jewish communities enjoyed. In Rome, Emperor Claudius had expelled Jews around AD 49, and Emperor Nero turned violently on Christians after the great fire of AD 64.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (59, 'Traditionally, this letter was written by James, a brother of Jesus, who did not follow him during his ministry but became a believer after the resurrection. He went on to lead the church in Jerusalem and was respected among Jewish believers for his devotion to God''s law. He was killed in Jerusalem around AD 62. Some scholars think a later follower wrote in his name to honour his teaching. Others defend the traditional view.', 'The letter is addressed to God''s people scattered across the world. Traditionally this is taken to mean Jewish believers in Jesus living outside their homeland across the Roman world; some scholars think it describes all Christians, Jewish and Gentile alike, as God''s people spread abroad. Many seem to have been poor, facing hardship and mistreatment, and sometimes pushed around by wealthy landowners and powerful neighbours.', 'If James wrote it, the letter likely dates from the 40s or 50s AD, making it one of the earliest Christian writings. Some scholars date it later in the first century. Judea and the surrounding lands were under Roman rule, with rising tension between Jews and their Roman governors. In many places a small group of rich landowners held most of the land, while ordinary people worked as day labourers or small farmers.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (60, 'The letter names Simon Peter as its writer. He was a fisherman from Galilee, in northern Israel, who became one of Jesus'' twelve closest disciples and later a leading figure in the early church. Many scholars instead think a follower of Peter wrote it in his name after his death, drawing on his teaching. The letter also mentions Silvanus, who may have helped write or carry it.', 'The letter is addressed to Christians scattered across five regions of what is now Turkey, then part of the Roman Empire. Many seem to have been non-Jews who had left their old religious and social customs behind. Because of this, neighbours treated them with suspicion, slander and hostility, and they lived like outsiders in their own towns.', 'The Roman Empire ruled the whole Mediterranean world, and everyday life was bound up with honouring the emperor and the local gods. Christians who refused to take part could be seen as odd, disloyal or even dangerous. Tradition dates the letter to the early 60s AD, shortly before Peter was put to death in Rome under the emperor Nero; many scholars prefer a date between about AD 70 and 90.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (61, 'The letter names its writer as Simon Peter, a fisherman from Galilee whom Jesus called to be one of his twelve closest followers, known as apostles. Peter became a leading figure in the early church, preaching in Jerusalem and travelling widely. The letter presents itself as written near the end of his life. Many scholars think a follower wrote it in Peter''s name after his death, because its style differs from 1 Peter; the traditional view holds that Peter wrote it himself, perhaps with a helper.', 'It was written to Christians who had already received an earlier letter from Peter, probably the believers in what is now Turkey addressed in 1 Peter. Teachers had arisen among them who mocked the hope that Jesus would return and encouraged careless, self-indulgent living.', 'The Roman Empire ruled the Mediterranean world, and Greek language and ideas shaped daily life in its cities. If Peter wrote the letter, it dates to the mid-60s AD under Emperor Nero, shortly before Peter''s death in Rome, where tradition says he was crucified. Those who see it as written later place it near the end of the first century or early in the second. Either way, the first generation of Jesus'' followers was dying, and some Christians wondered why he had not yet come back.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (62, 'The letter never names its writer, but from the second century Christians have traditionally credited it to John the apostle, a Galilean fisherman and son of Zebedee who became one of Jesus'' closest disciples. Early tradition says John spent his old age leading churches around Ephesus, in what is now western Turkey. Many scholars instead think it was written by a respected leader in a circle of churches shaped by John''s teaching, possibly the figure later known as ''John the Elder.'' Whoever he was, he writes as an older, trusted pastor who claims a close link to the first eyewitnesses of Jesus.', 'It was most likely sent to a group of house churches, probably in or around Ephesus in the Roman province called Asia (a region on the western edge of modern Turkey, not the continent), whose members the writer knew well and cared for. These congregations had recently been shaken by a painful split over what to believe about Jesus, and those who remained were unsettled and needed reassurance.', 'It is usually dated to around AD 90–100, though some place it a little earlier. The Roman Empire was at peace and its cities were full of temples to many gods, while the Jerusalem temple had been destroyed in AD 70, some twenty to thirty years earlier, and most of the original apostles had died. Christianity was spreading in Greek-speaking cities, and some teachers there were blending the faith with popular ideas that the physical world was inferior to the spiritual.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (63, 'The writer calls himself simply "the elder," a title for a respected senior leader who cared for a group of churches. Church tradition identifies him as John the apostle, a former fisherman from Galilee who became one of Jesus'' closest followers and, in old age, a leader of churches around Ephesus. Many scholars instead think he was a different John, known as John the Elder, a leader in the same region. Either way, the letter shares its language and concerns with 1 John and 3 John.', 'The letter is addressed to "the chosen lady and her children." Most think this is a warm way of naming a particular local church and its members, though some think it was a real woman and her family who hosted believers in her home. The church faced travelling teachers whose ideas about Jesus differed from what the elder had taught.', 'It was likely written in the late first century AD, perhaps around AD 90, when the Roman Empire ruled the Mediterranean world and Christians were a small, scattered minority. Churches met in private homes, and travelling preachers moved from town to town, relying on believers to give them food and lodging. Some of these teachers claimed that Jesus had not truly come as a flesh-and-blood human, an idea that was dividing congregations in the region.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (64, 'The writer calls himself simply "the elder," a title for a respected older leader who cared for a group of churches. Since early times Christians have identified him as John, one of Jesus'' twelve disciples and a former fisherman from Galilee, who is said to have spent his later years in Ephesus. Some scholars think the elder was a different leader, sometimes called John the Elder, who belonged to the same circle of churches. The letter shares its style and outlook with 1 and 2 John, and most readers see one hand behind all three.', 'This is a short personal letter to one man, Gaius, a believer the elder loved and trusted. He was part of a local church, probably in the Roman province of Asia (western Turkey), that met in a home. The elder had heard good reports of how Gaius welcomed and cared for visiting Christian workers.', 'The letter is usually dated to the late first century AD, perhaps around AD 90, when the Roman Empire ruled the Mediterranean world. Most churches were small groups meeting in private houses, and travelling preachers and teachers moved between them. With no church buildings or salaries, these travellers depended on believers to give them food, lodging and help for the next stage of their journey. Letters were carried by hand, often by a trusted traveller who could add news in person.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (65, 'The writer calls himself Jude, a servant of Jesus Christ and brother of James, usually taken to be the James who led the church in Jerusalem and was himself a brother of Jesus. Traditionally Jude is identified as one of the brothers of Jesus named in the Gospels. Jesus'' brothers did not believe in him during his ministry, but they are found among the believers after the resurrection, and Paul mentions the Lord''s brothers travelling as missionaries. Beyond this, little is known about Jude himself. Many scholars accept the traditional view, while others think a later follower wrote in Jude''s name to carry his authority.', 'The letter does not name its readers. They were probably believers steeped in Jewish Scripture and tradition, perhaps in Palestine or nearby, meeting in small house churches. Certain people had slipped into their gatherings, sharing their meals while living immorally and treating God''s grace as a licence for it, and Jude writes urgently to warn the believers about them.', 'Scholars date Jude anywhere from around AD 50–70, if Jude himself wrote it, to around AD 90 or a little later, if it was written in his name. The young church was spreading across the Roman Empire through small household congregations, often visited by travelling teachers whose claims were hard to check. Letters like this one were carried by messengers and read aloud when believers gathered.');
insert into public.bible_book_settings (book_number, author, audience, era) values (66, 'The writer calls himself John, a servant of Jesus who shared in the hardships of the believers he wrote to. Christian tradition from the second century identifies him as the apostle John, a former Galilean fisherman and one of Jesus'' twelve closest followers, who later led churches around Ephesus. Many scholars think he was a different John, a respected Christian prophet in the same region, noting that his Greek style differs from John''s Gospel. He wrote in a vivid style full of visions and symbols, drawing heavily on Old Testament prophets like Daniel and Ezekiel.', 'It was first sent to seven churches in the Roman province of Asia, in what is now western Turkey, and was meant to be read aloud to each congregation. These were small groups of believers in busy, prosperous cities, some facing hostility, poverty or prison, others growing comfortable and blending in with the surrounding culture.', 'Most scholars date the book to around AD 95, under the Roman emperor Domitian; some place it earlier, in the late 60s, after Emperor Nero''s persecution of Christians in Rome. Rome ruled the whole Mediterranean world, and cities in Asia competed to honour the emperor with temples and festivals. Christians who refused to take part in emperor worship or in trade-guild feasts dedicated to pagan gods could face suspicion, lost business or worse. If the book was written around AD 95, Jerusalem''s temple had already been destroyed by Roman armies in AD 70; on the earlier date, the war with Rome that led to its destruction was under way or about to begin.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (1, 1, 1, 'Neighbouring peoples, especially the Babylonians, had their own creation stories, involving many gods battling one another; Israel''s first hearers would likely have known such tales.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (1, 10, 1, 'Many names in this list match peoples and places known from the ancient world, such as Egypt, Canaan, Assyria and the islands and coasts of the Mediterranean.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (1, 11, 1, 'Babel is the Hebrew name for Babylon. Babylon and other cities of Mesopotamia built ziggurats, tall stepped towers of mud brick faced with baked brick, with temples to their gods at the top.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (1, 14, 1, 'Salem, the city of the priest-king Melchizedek, has long been identified with Jerusalem, which later became Israel''s capital.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (1, 23, 1, 'The cave of Machpelah lies at Hebron, south of Jerusalem; a site there, known as the Tomb of the Patriarchs, is still honoured by Jews, Christians and Muslims.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (1, 28, 10, 'Bethel, a town north of Jerusalem, later became one of the main places of worship in the northern kingdom of Israel.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (1, 41, 1, 'Egypt''s harvests depended on the Nile flooding every year; when the flood failed, famine followed, so dreams about the river and its cattle would have felt deeply serious.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (1, 49, 1, 'Jacob''s blessing is a poem, and many scholars consider it among the oldest pieces of poetry in the Bible. It has a section for each of Jacob''s twelve sons, whose descendants became the tribes of Israel.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (2, 1, 1, 'Later in this passage the Israelites are forced to build Pithom and Rameses, store cities in the eastern Nile Delta, near the region of Goshen where the Israelites lived.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (2, 2, 16, 'Midian was a desert region east of the Gulf of Aqaba, in what is now north-western Saudi Arabia, home to wandering herding clans.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (2, 3, 1, 'Horeb is another name for Mount Sinai; its exact location is debated, traditionally placed at Jebel Musa in southern Sinai, though some suggest north-western Arabia.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (2, 12, 1, 'This marks the start of Passover, the spring festival Israel kept every year in the month of Abib, roughly March to April.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (2, 13, 17, 'The shorter route through Philistine territory was the main coastal highway to Canaan, guarded by a line of Egyptian army forts.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (2, 14, 1, 'The sea Israel camps beside is called in Hebrew Yam Suph, usually translated "Red Sea" but literally "Sea of Reeds"; its exact location, whether a lake, marsh or arm of the Red Sea, is uncertain.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (2, 15, 1, 'Many scholars regard this victory song, sung by Moses and then by his sister Miriam with tambourines, as one of the oldest poems in the Bible.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (2, 17, 8, 'The Amalekites were a nomadic desert people of the Sinai and the dry Negev region south of Canaan.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (2, 19, 1, 'Israel arrives at Mount Sinai in the third month after leaving Egypt, roughly seven weeks after setting out, and camps there for nearly a year, until the people move on in Numbers chapter 10.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (2, 20, 1, 'Scholars note that this covenant (a binding agreement) resembles ancient treaties in which a great king set out terms for the people under his protection.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (2, 21, 1, 'The laws from the end of chapter 20 through chapter 23 are often called the "Book of the Covenant"; they resemble other ancient codes, such as Babylon''s famous laws of King Hammurabi.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (2, 23, 10, 'Later in this passage three yearly festivals are named; they followed the farming calendar: barley harvest in spring, wheat harvest in early summer, and fruit gathering in autumn.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (2, 25, 1, 'The tabernacle was a portable tent-shrine that could be taken apart and carried as the Israelites travelled; acacia wood grows in the Sinai desert.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (2, 25, 10, 'Cherubim were winged guardian figures; similar winged creatures guarding thrones and sacred places appear in Egyptian and other ancient Near Eastern art.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (2, 28, 15, 'The Urim and Thummim were objects kept in the high priest''s breastpiece for seeking God''s decisions; no one today knows exactly what they looked like.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (2, 30, 11, 'A shekel was a weight of silver, roughly 11 grams; this half-shekel payment later became the yearly temple tax still collected in Jesus'' day.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (2, 32, 1, 'Bull or calf images were common symbols of divine strength in Egypt and Canaan; centuries later King Jeroboam set up golden calves at Bethel and Dan.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (2, 38, 21, 'A talent was a large weight of metal, roughly 34 kilograms, so these totals describe a very great quantity of gold, silver and bronze.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (2, 40, 17, 'The tabernacle is set up on the first day of the second year after leaving Egypt, almost exactly a year after the first Passover.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (3, 8, 1, 'This ceremony carries out the instructions for ordaining Aaron and his sons that Moses had received earlier on the mountain, recorded in Exodus 29.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (3, 13, 1, 'The Hebrew word often translated "leprosy" covered a range of skin conditions, and even mould in cloth, not just the disease we call leprosy today.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (3, 14, 33, 'Living in tents at the time, the Israelites are given rules looking ahead to settled life in stone houses in Canaan.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (3, 16, 1, 'This describes the yearly Day of Atonement, still observed by Jewish people today as Yom Kippur, usually falling in September or October.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (3, 17, 1, 'Scholars often call chapters 17 to 26 the "Holiness Code", a distinct collection of laws that may once have circulated on its own.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (3, 23, 15, 'This harvest feast, the Feast of Weeks, came fifty days after Passover, and Greek-speaking Jews later called it Pentecost, from the Greek word for fiftieth.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (3, 23, 23, 'This autumn day of trumpet blasts later developed into Rosh Hashanah, the Jewish New Year.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (3, 23, 33, 'The Feast of Booths, or Tabernacles, involved living in leafy shelters for a week; Jewish families still celebrate it today as Sukkot.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (3, 25, 8, 'Whether the Jubilee, every fiftieth year, was ever fully practised is uncertain; the Old Testament records no clear example of it being kept.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (3, 26, 1, 'Ending a set of laws with blessings and curses echoes ancient Near Eastern treaties between kings and their subjects, which usually closed the same way.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (3, 26, 34, 'Centuries later, the writer of 2 Chronicles connected this passage to the land lying empty during the Babylonian exile.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (3, 27, 1, 'Many readers see this chapter on vows as an appendix, since the previous chapter closes with what sounds like the book''s natural ending.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (4, 6, 22, 'Two tiny silver scrolls found in a tomb near Jerusalem, dated around 600 BC, carry a version of this blessing, the oldest surviving copy of any words found in the Bible.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (4, 12, 1, 'Cush was a land south of Egypt, roughly modern Sudan; some readers think the wife meant here is Zipporah, others a second wife.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (4, 20, 1, 'Most readers understand the story here as jumping forward about thirty-eight years, from the second year after leaving Egypt to near the fortieth.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (4, 20, 14, 'Edom lay south-east of the Dead Sea; its people were seen as descendants of Esau, Jacob''s brother, and the King''s Highway was a major trade route through it.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (4, 20, 22, 'Later in Numbers, Aaron''s death on Mount Hor is dated to the fifth month of the fortieth year after leaving Egypt.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (4, 21, 4, 'Centuries later, around 700 BC, King Hezekiah destroyed a bronze snake kept in Jerusalem, said to be the one Moses made; it was called Nehushtan, and people had been offering incense to it.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (4, 21, 10, 'This passage quotes the Book of the Wars of the Lord, an ancient collection of songs or poems that has since been lost.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (4, 21, 33, 'Bashan was a fertile highland east of the Sea of Galilee, famous in ancient times for its rich pastures, strong cattle and oak trees.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (4, 22, 1, 'An inscription found at Deir Alla in Jordan, dated around 800 BC, mentions a seer named Balaam son of Beor, showing he was remembered beyond Israel.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (4, 25, 1, 'Baal was the chief storm and fertility god worshipped by Canaanites and neighbours; Baal of Peor was the local form honoured on a mountain called Peor in Moab.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (4, 28, 26, 'The Festival of Weeks came seven weeks after Passover at the wheat harvest; Greek-speaking Jews later called it Pentecost, meaning fiftieth.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (4, 29, 1, 'This first-day-of-the-seventh-month gathering, marked by trumpet blasts, became the Jewish New Year, Rosh Hashanah, in later times.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (4, 29, 7, 'The tenth day of the seventh month is the Day of Atonement, Yom Kippur, Israel''s most solemn day of fasting and cleansing.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (4, 29, 12, 'This week-long feast is the Festival of Shelters, or Tabernacles, an autumn harvest celebration when Israelites lived in temporary huts for the week.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (4, 31, 1, 'The Midianites were a desert people living around the edges of Israel''s route; Moses'' own wife Zipporah and father-in-law Jethro were Midianites.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (4, 32, 1, 'Gilead and Jazer were rich grazing lands east of the Jordan River, in what is now Jordan, outside the land of Canaan proper.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (4, 33, 1, 'This list of camping places traces the whole desert journey; many of the sites named can no longer be identified with certainty today.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (4, 35, 9, 'The book of Joshua later names the six cities of refuge, three on each side of the Jordan River.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (5, 1, 1, 'The book dates these speeches to the eleventh month of the fortieth year after leaving Egypt, delivered in the land of Moab east of the Jordan.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (5, 2, 1, 'The Edomites were descended from Esau, Jacob''s brother, so Israel regarded them as relatives; Moab and Ammon traced their ancestry to Abraham''s nephew Lot.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (5, 3, 1, 'Bashan was a fertile highland region east of the Sea of Galilee, famous for its cattle and oak forests, covering today''s Golan Heights and the lands east of it in southern Syria.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (5, 5, 1, 'Horeb is Deuteronomy''s usual name for Mount Sinai, where the law was given about forty years earlier.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (5, 12, 1, 'In this passage Israel is told to bring its sacrifices to one place God would choose, rather than to the many local shrines. That place was later understood to be Jerusalem; many scholars link these laws to King Josiah closing local shrines around 622 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (5, 16, 1, 'This chapter sets out Israel''s three yearly pilgrim festivals, tied to spring and autumn harvests: Passover, the Feast of Weeks (later called Pentecost) and the Feast of Shelters (also called Tabernacles or Booths).');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (5, 25, 17, 'The Amalekites were a desert people who attacked Israel soon after the escape from Egypt, picking off stragglers at the rear.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (5, 27, 1, 'Mount Ebal and Mount Gerizim are twin peaks overlooking the town of Shechem in central Canaan, where Abraham had first camped in the land.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (5, 28, 15, 'The curses in this chapter, especially later on, closely resemble those in Assyrian treaties, including one made by King Esarhaddon in 672 BC; other scholars point to similarities with older Hittite treaties as well.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (5, 31, 24, 'Here Moses finishes writing down the law, and the scroll is placed beside the ark of the covenant, the sacred gold-covered chest the Israelites carried with them. Later in this passage Moses begins a long song, recited before the whole assembly.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (5, 33, 1, 'This farewell blessing on the tribes of Israel echoes the deathbed blessing Jacob spoke over his twelve sons in Genesis, though the tribe of Simeon is not named here.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (5, 34, 1, 'Mount Nebo, in today''s Jordan, overlooks the Jordan valley and Dead Sea; Jewish tradition credits Joshua with writing this account of Moses'' death.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (6, 5, 13, 'Jericho, an oasis town just north of the Dead Sea, is among the oldest inhabited places on earth; archaeologists still debate when its ancient walls fell.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (6, 7, 1, 'Ai''s name means ''the ruin''; it is often placed at a site called et-Tell near Bethel, though that identification and its dating are debated.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (6, 8, 30, 'Mount Ebal overlooks Shechem in the central hills; a stone structure found there in the 1980s was identified by its excavator as an altar, though others disagree.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (6, 9, 1, 'Gibeon lay a few miles north of Jerusalem; excavations there uncovered jar handles inscribed with the town''s name.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (6, 10, 1, 'The Book of Jashar, mentioned later in this passage, was an ancient collection of Israelite poems, now lost, which is also quoted in 2 Samuel.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (6, 11, 1, 'Hazor, in the far north, was the largest city in Canaan; excavations found it destroyed by a great fire in the 1200s BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (6, 13, 1, 'The five Philistine cities named in this passage lay on the southern coastal plain. The Philistines were seafaring newcomers, probably from the Aegean region; most historians think their main settlement came around 1200 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (6, 15, 45, 'Jerusalem, mentioned at the end of this passage, stayed in Jebusite hands until King David captured it around 1000 BC and made it his capital.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (6, 18, 1, 'Shiloh, in the hills of Ephraim, became Israel''s main place of worship and remained so until the days of the prophet Samuel.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (6, 19, 40, 'The tribe of Dan''s move north to capture Leshem, also called Laish, is told in fuller detail in Judges 18.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (6, 24, 1, 'Shechem, between Mounts Ebal and Gerizim, was where Abraham first built an altar in Canaan and where Jacob later bought land.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (7, 1, 21, 'Jerusalem, home of a people called the Jebusites, was never securely held by Israel in this period; King David finally captured it generations later. The passage then turns to the town of Bethel, once called Luz.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (7, 5, 1, 'The Song of Deborah is widely regarded as one of the oldest pieces of Hebrew poetry in the Bible, likely composed close to the events it celebrates.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (7, 13, 1, 'The Philistines, newcomers from the Aegean region (the seas around Greece), ruled five coastal cities: Gaza, Ashkelon, Ashdod, Gath and Ekron.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (7, 18, 11, 'Near the end of this passage, the writer mentions priests serving at Dan until the land''s captivity. This most likely refers to the Assyrian conquest of northern Israel in the 700s BC, when many people were carried off, so many readers take it as a clue that the book was completed after that time.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (7, 19, 1, 'Later in this passage the travellers stop for the night at Gibeah, a town in the tribe of Benjamin, which later became the hometown of Saul, Israel''s first king.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (8, 2, 1, 'Israel''s law told landowners to leave grain at the field edges and not gather what reapers dropped, so poor people, foreigners and widows could collect it, called gleaning.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (8, 2, 14, 'Later in this passage Naomi calls Boaz one of the family''s redeemers: a close male relative who had the right to buy back family land and protect the family name.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (8, 3, 1, 'A threshing floor was a hard, open patch where harvested grain was beaten and tossed into the evening breeze to separate it from husks; workers often slept there to guard it.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (8, 4, 1, 'The town gate was where elders met to settle legal business in public. A close male relative, called a kinsman-redeemer, had the right to buy back family land and protect the family name.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (9, 1, 1, 'Shiloh, in the hill country north of Jerusalem, was then Israel''s main place of worship, where Israel''s sanctuary stood and the ark of the covenant was kept.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (9, 4, 2, 'The ark of the covenant was a gold-covered wooden chest holding the stone tablets of the law, Israel''s most sacred object, seen as the place of God''s presence.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (9, 4, 12, 'Shiloh itself seems to have been destroyed around this time, since the prophet Jeremiah, and also Psalm 78, later recalled its ruin as a warning.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (9, 5, 1, 'Ashdod was one of the five chief Philistine cities, along with Gaza, Ashkelon, Gath and Ekron; Dagon was a god the Philistines worshipped.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (9, 15, 1, 'The Amalekites were desert raiders of the far south, remembered as Israel''s enemies since they attacked the Israelites on their journey out of Egypt.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (9, 19, 8, 'The heading of Psalm 59 links that psalm to the night Saul sent men to watch David''s house to kill him.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (9, 21, 10, 'The headings of Psalms 34 and 56 link them to David''s time in the Philistine city of Gath. Psalm 34''s heading calls the king Abimelech, though this passage names him Achish; Abimelech may have been a royal title.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (9, 22, 6, 'The heading of Psalm 52 links it to Doeg the Edomite reporting to Saul that David had visited the priests at Nob.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (9, 23, 19, 'Psalm 54''s heading links it to the men of Ziph betraying David; Psalms 57 and 142 are traditionally tied to his hiding in a cave, either at Adullam or at En Gedi.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (9, 25, 1, 'The Carmel mentioned in this passage was a small town in the hill country of Judah, south of Hebron, not the well-known Mount Carmel in the north.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (10, 1, 17, 'This lament is said to come from the Book of Jashar, an ancient collection of Israelite poems and songs that has since been lost.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (10, 2, 1, 'Hebron, an old town in the hills of Judah, was linked with Abraham''s family burial place and became David''s first capital.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (10, 2, 12, 'Archaeologists have uncovered a large rock-cut pool at el-Jib, north of Jerusalem, which is often linked with the pool of Gibeon.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (10, 5, 6, 'Jerusalem was held by the Jebusites, a Canaanite people. It sat on the border between the tribes of Benjamin and Judah, and the Israelites had never managed to drive the Jebusites out.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (10, 6, 1, 'The ark was a gold-covered chest holding the covenant stone tablets; it had stayed at Kiriath Jearim, here called by its other name, Baale Judah, for years after the Philistines returned it.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (10, 8, 15, 'This passage lists David''s chief officials. Among them, the Kerethites and Pelethites were probably foreign soldiers serving as the king''s bodyguard, possibly with links to Crete and the Philistines.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (10, 11, 1, 'Spring, after the winter rains, was the usual season for war; Rabbah, the Ammonite capital, lies under modern Amman in Jordan.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (10, 21, 1, 'Many scholars see chapters 21–24 as a collection of extra stories, lists and poems, not in time order; the Gibeonites were Canaanites protected by an old treaty.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (10, 22, 1, 'This song appears again, almost word for word, as Psalm 18, whose heading also links it to David''s rescue from Saul and his enemies.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (10, 24, 18, 'The later book of 2 Chronicles names this threshing floor, an open flat area for separating grain, as the place where Solomon built the temple.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (11, 1, 28, 'The Gihon spring, where Solomon is anointed, was the city''s main water source, in the Kidron Valley just below the oldest part of Jerusalem, known as the City of David.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (11, 2, 26, 'Anathoth, where Abiathar is sent, was a town of priests a few miles north-east of Jerusalem; centuries later it was the prophet Jeremiah''s home town.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (11, 3, 4, 'Gibeon was a town a few miles north-west of Jerusalem; according to Chronicles, the old tent of worship from Moses'' time stood there.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (11, 5, 1, 'Hiram ruled Tyre, a wealthy Phoenician port in what is now Lebanon, with access to the famous cedar forests of the Lebanon mountains inland.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (11, 6, 1, 'Building began in Solomon''s fourth year, usually dated around 966 BC; the month Ziv falls in spring, around April or May.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (11, 8, 1, 'The dedication took place in the seventh month, autumn, at the Festival of Shelters (Tabernacles), a week-long harvest celebration when crowds gathered in Jerusalem.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (11, 9, 15, 'Archaeologists have found large city gateways at Hazor, Megiddo and Gezer; some date them to Solomon''s time, while others argue they were built later.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (11, 9, 24, 'Ezion-geber lay near Elath at the tip of the Gulf of Aqaba, giving Solomon a port onto the Red Sea.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (11, 10, 1, 'Sheba is usually identified with a kingdom in south-west Arabia, modern Yemen, known for the incense trade; Ethiopian tradition links the queen with Ethiopia.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (11, 11, 23, 'Damascus was the capital of Aram, a kingdom roughly in modern Syria that became Israel''s frequent rival to the north.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (11, 12, 1, 'The split of the kingdom is usually dated around 930 BC; Shechem was an ancient meeting place in the central hill country.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (11, 12, 25, 'Dan and Bethel stood near the northern and southern edges of Jeroboam''s new kingdom, giving his people places to worship without travelling to Jerusalem.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (11, 14, 21, 'Shishak is usually identified with Pharaoh Sheshonq I, whose own temple inscription in Egypt lists a military campaign through this region around 925 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (11, 16, 21, 'Omri founded Samaria as Israel''s capital; an ancient Moabite inscription names him, and Assyrian records later called Israel ''the house of Omri''.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (11, 16, 29, 'Ahab reigned around 874 to 853 BC; an Assyrian inscription records him sending 2,000 chariots against Assyria''s king at the battle of Qarqar in 853 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (11, 17, 7, 'Zarephath was a coastal town near Sidon in Phoenicia, the homeland of Queen Jezebel and a centre of Baal worship.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (11, 18, 16, 'Mount Carmel is a ridge running to the Mediterranean coast near modern Haifa, on the borderland between Israel and Phoenicia.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (11, 19, 1, 'Beersheba lay at Judah''s southern edge; Horeb, Elijah''s destination far to the south, is another name for Mount Sinai, where Moses received the law.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (11, 21, 1, 'Jezreel, in a fertile valley north of Samaria, was the site of a second royal residence for Ahab''s family.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (11, 22, 1, 'Ramoth-gilead was a fortified town east of the Jordan, on the disputed border between Israel and Aram.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (12, 3, 4, 'A Moabite inscription known as the Mesha Stone, found in 1868, records King Mesha''s own account of throwing off Israel''s rule.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (12, 8, 7, 'Assyrian inscriptions also mention Hazael, describing him as a man of no royal family, which matches his seizing the throne of Aram, the kingdom based at Damascus.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (12, 9, 14, 'An Aramean inscription found at Tel Dan, probably set up by Hazael, claims credit for killing Joram and Ahaziah; scholars debate how it fits with this account.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (12, 10, 29, 'An Assyrian monument called the Black Obelisk shows Jehu or his envoy bowing and paying tribute to King Shalmaneser III around 841 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (12, 14, 23, 'The prophets Amos and Hosea preached in Israel during Jeroboam II''s long, prosperous reign.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (12, 15, 1, 'Azariah is also called Uzziah; the prophet Isaiah dates his call to the year this king died, around 740 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (12, 15, 17, 'Pul is the Assyrian king Tiglath-pileser III, whose own records list Menahem of Samaria among the kings paying him tribute.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (12, 15, 27, 'Tiglath-pileser III''s records claim that after Pekah was overthrown, the Assyrian king himself placed Hoshea on Israel''s throne.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (12, 15, 32, 'The prophets Isaiah and Micah were active in Judah around Jotham''s reign.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (12, 16, 1, 'This crisis, around 734 BC, is the setting of the prophet Isaiah''s meeting with King Ahaz described in Isaiah 7.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (12, 17, 1, 'Samaria fell around 722 BC; the Assyrian king Sargon II later claimed in his inscriptions to have captured it and deported its people.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (12, 17, 24, 'Jewish tradition traced the later Samaritans partly to these resettled peoples, though the Samaritans themselves trace their roots to the old northern tribes.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (12, 18, 13, 'This invasion took place in 701 BC; Sennacherib''s own annals describe the campaign and boast of shutting Hezekiah up in Jerusalem.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (12, 18, 17, 'Lachish, Sennacherib''s base, was besieged and taken; carved stone panels in his palace at Nineveh show the siege. Isaiah 36–37 tells this same story.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (12, 19, 35, 'Sennacherib''s murder, told at the end of this passage, is also recorded in a Babylonian chronicle, which says he was killed by his son in a rebellion in 681 BC, about twenty years after this campaign.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (12, 20, 12, 'Merodach-baladan was a Babylonian leader who repeatedly rebelled against Assyria, so his envoys may have been seeking allies.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (12, 20, 20, 'Hezekiah''s water tunnel still runs beneath Jerusalem; an inscription carved by its diggers was discovered inside it in 1880.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (12, 21, 1, 'Assyrian records list Manasseh of Judah among the subject kings who sent tribute and building materials to Assyrian rulers.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (12, 22, 1, 'The prophets Jeremiah and Zephaniah began their work during Josiah''s reign, which started around 640 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (12, 22, 8, 'This discovery came around 622 BC; many scholars think the scroll found was some form of the book of Deuteronomy.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (12, 23, 28, 'In 609 BC Pharaoh Necho was marching to help the failing Assyrian empire against the rising power of Babylon when Josiah confronted him.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (12, 23, 36, 'Babylon defeated Egypt at Carchemish in 605 BC, bringing Judah under Babylonian control; Jeremiah 36 tells of Jehoiakim burning the prophet''s scroll.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (12, 24, 8, 'A Babylonian chronicle dates this capture of Jerusalem to early 597 BC; the prophet Ezekiel was among those taken to Babylon.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (12, 24, 18, 'Throughout Zedekiah''s reign the prophet Jeremiah urged surrender to Babylon, as the book of Jeremiah records.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (12, 25, 8, 'Jerusalem and its temple fell in 586 BC (some date it 587); the book of Lamentations traditionally mourns this destruction.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (12, 25, 22, 'Jeremiah 40–43 tells this episode more fully, including the prophet himself being taken to Egypt afterwards.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (12, 25, 27, 'Evil-merodach (Amel-Marduk) began ruling around 562 BC. Years earlier, Babylonian ration tablets already listed Jehoiachin as king of Judah and recorded food allowances for him and his sons while he was a captive.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (13, 1, 1, 'The opening family lists, from Adam onward, are drawn largely from the book of Genesis, compressed into names alone.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (13, 3, 17, 'Jeconiah, also called Jehoiachin, was the king taken captive to Babylon in 597 BC; this list traces his descendants several generations beyond the exile.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (13, 5, 23, 'Later in this passage comes Tiglath-pileser III, also called Pul, the Assyrian king who carried off the tribes east of the Jordan around the 730s BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (13, 6, 1, 'The priestly line ends with Jehozadak, exiled in 586 BC; his son Joshua later became high priest among those who returned and rebuilt the temple.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (13, 9, 1, 'This list of people resettling Jerusalem after the exile closely parallels a similar list in Nehemiah 11.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (13, 10, 1, 'Saul''s final battle on Mount Gilboa, in northern Israel, is usually dated around 1010 BC; the account parallels 1 Samuel 31.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (13, 11, 4, 'David''s capture of Jerusalem, then a Jebusite (Canaanite) stronghold, is usually dated around 1000 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (13, 13, 1, 'The ark, Israel''s sacred gold-covered chest, had stayed at Kiriath-jearim for many years, largely neglected in Saul''s day, ever since the Philistines returned it.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (13, 16, 7, 'This song of thanks shares its opening section with Psalm 105.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (13, 16, 23, 'This part of the song matches Psalm 96, with closing lines found in Psalm 106.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (13, 19, 1, 'The Ammonites lived east of the Jordan River, around the city of Rabbah, today''s Amman in Jordan.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (13, 21, 18, 'Ornan''s threshing floor, where grain was separated from husks, became the site of Solomon''s temple, which Chronicles elsewhere links with Mount Moriah.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (13, 24, 1, 'These twenty-four priestly divisions were still serving in turns in New Testament times; Zechariah, father of John the Baptist, belonged to Abijah''s division.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (13, 25, 1, 'Asaph, Heman and Jeduthun are names also found in the headings of several psalms, linking those songs with these temple musician families.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (13, 29, 26, 'David''s reign is usually dated around 1010–970 BC. The records of Samuel, Nathan and Gad mentioned here do not survive as separate books, though some scholars think they refer to material now in 1 and 2 Samuel.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (14, 3, 1, 'Solomon began building the temple in his fourth year as king, usually dated around 966 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (14, 9, 1, 'Sheba is widely thought to have been in southwest Arabia, around modern Yemen, a kingdom grown rich on the trade in incense and spices.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (14, 10, 1, 'This meeting at Shechem, an ancient gathering place of the northern tribes, is usually dated around 930 BC, when the kingdom split in two.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (14, 12, 1, 'Shishak is the Egyptian pharaoh Sheshonq I, whose invasion around 925 BC is recorded in a list of conquered towns carved on a temple wall in Egypt.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (14, 26, 16, 'Uzziah died around 740 BC; the prophet Isaiah began his work around the year of his death.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (14, 28, 16, 'The Assyrian king here is Tiglath-pileser III, who around 734 BC was expanding his empire westward across Syria and Israel.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (14, 30, 1, 'Assyria had been carrying off people from the northern kingdom of Israel and conquered it completely around 722 BC, so Hezekiah''s messengers were inviting those left behind in its land.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (14, 32, 1, 'The Assyrian king Sennacherib invaded Judah in 701 BC; his own records boast of shutting Hezekiah up in Jerusalem ''like a bird in a cage''.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (14, 32, 27, 'The water tunnel Hezekiah dug under Jerusalem, mentioned later in this passage, still survives and can be walked through today; an ancient inscription found inside describes its digging.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (14, 33, 10, 'Assyrian records name Manasseh among kings paying tribute to Assyria; his captivity and return are told only in Chronicles.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (14, 34, 14, 'This discovery took place around 622 BC; many scholars think the book found was an early form of Deuteronomy.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (14, 35, 20, 'In 609 BC Pharaoh Necho II was marching north to help the failing Assyrian Empire against Babylon when Josiah confronted him at Megiddo.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (14, 36, 15, 'The Babylonian army under Nebuchadnezzar destroyed Jerusalem and its temple in 586 BC, and many survivors were taken into exile in Babylon.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (14, 36, 22, 'Cyrus, king of Persia, conquered Babylon in 539 BC and soon let exiles return home; the book of Ezra opens with these same words.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (15, 1, 1, 'Cyrus issued this decree in his first year ruling Babylon, around 538 BC; a surviving clay cylinder records his policy of letting conquered peoples return and restore their shrines.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (15, 1, 5, 'Later in this passage, Sheshbazzar, called the prince of Judah, is named as the leader of this first return; some identify him with Zerubbabel, while others think he was a different leader, perhaps a relative.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (15, 2, 1, 'This list of returning families appears again, with small differences, in Nehemiah chapter 7.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (15, 3, 1, 'The seventh month fell in early autumn, the season of the Festival of Shelters; Zerubbabel descended from Judah''s royal family, and Jeshua was the high priest.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (15, 3, 7, 'The temple foundation was laid around 536 BC, with cedar from Lebanon supplied by the people of the coastal cities of Tyre and Sidon and brought by sea to the port of Joppa, as had been done for Solomon''s temple.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (15, 4, 1, 'These neighbours descended partly from peoples whom Assyrian kings had resettled in the north after 722 BC; they are often linked with the later Samaritans.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (15, 4, 6, 'This section jumps ahead to later kings, Xerxes and Artaxerxes, and concerns Jerusalem''s walls; from 4:8 the text switches to Aramaic, the empire''s official language.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (15, 4, 24, 'Work resumed in the second year of King Darius I, around 520 BC, urged on by the prophets Haggai and Zechariah, whose own books appear later in the Old Testament.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (15, 5, 3, 'Tattenai governed the large Persian province west of the Euphrates River, called Beyond the River; a Babylonian document from this period also names him as governor.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (15, 6, 1, 'Cyrus''s record was found at Ecbatana, a Persian royal city in the hills of what is now western Iran, where kings often spent the summer.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (15, 6, 13, 'The temple was finished in Darius''s sixth year, around 515 BC, roughly seventy years after Babylon destroyed Solomon''s temple in 586 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (15, 6, 19, 'The text returns to Hebrew here; Passover, the spring festival remembering Israel''s escape from Egypt, was held in the first month of the year.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (15, 7, 1, 'Ezra travelled in the seventh year of Artaxerxes, traditionally dated 458 BC; some scholars instead place his journey in 398 BC, under a later Artaxerxes.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (15, 7, 11, 'This royal letter, written in Aramaic, gave Ezra official Persian authority to teach the law and appoint judges among the Jews of the province.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (15, 7, 27, 'Here the book shifts into Ezra''s own first-person account, often called his memoirs, which continues through chapter 9.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (15, 8, 15, 'The Ahava canal and the place called Casiphia were somewhere in Babylonia, but their exact locations are unknown.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (15, 8, 31, 'The journey from Babylonia to Jerusalem took about four months and covered roughly 900 miles along routes where travellers risked robbers.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (15, 10, 9, 'The ninth month fell around late November and December, the cold rainy season in Jerusalem, so the crowd gathered in the open square before the temple during winter rains.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (16, 1, 1, 'Nehemiah is at Susa, a royal Persian capital, in the month of Kislev (November–December), around 445 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (16, 2, 1, 'The month of Nisan (March–April) falls about four months after Nehemiah first heard the news from Jerusalem.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (16, 2, 9, 'Later in this passage three neighbouring leaders appear: Sanballat, governor of nearby Samaria, who is known also from ancient letters found in Egypt; Tobiah, an Ammonite official; and Geshem, an Arab leader.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (16, 5, 14, 'Nehemiah served as governor for twelve years, from about 445 to 433 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (16, 6, 15, 'The month of Elul falls in August–September, so the wall was finished in late summer, around 445 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (16, 7, 6, 'This list closely matches one in Ezra. It records families who returned from exile, with Zerubbabel among their leaders, in the decades after 538 BC, nearly a century earlier.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (16, 8, 1, 'The seventh month (September–October) held Israel''s major autumn festivals. Ezra was a priest and scribe, a scholar trained to copy and teach the law of Moses. Scholars debate whether he arrived in Jerusalem before Nehemiah, as traditionally held, or after.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (16, 8, 13, 'The Festival of Booths, or Tabernacles, was a week-long autumn harvest feast when families lived in shelters made of branches.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (16, 12, 1, 'These lists trace priestly families from the first return around 538 BC through several later generations of the Persian period.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (16, 13, 4, 'These events come after Nehemiah returned to the Persian king around 433 BC and later came back to Jerusalem.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (17, 1, 1, 'The great banquet described in this passage falls in the king''s third year, around 483 BC, a few years before Xerxes launched his invasion of Greece in 480 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (17, 2, 12, 'Later in this passage, Esther is brought to the king in his seventh year, around 479 BC, shortly after his costly and failed campaign against Greece.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (17, 3, 7, 'This is the king''s twelfth year, around 474 BC. ''Pur'' is an old Babylonian word for a lot, a small object cast to choose a day.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (17, 9, 20, 'This section records how the feast of Purim began. Jews still celebrate it each year in the month of Adar, around February or March.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (18, 1, 6, 'The Hebrew calls this figure ''the satan,'' meaning ''the accuser,'' who appears among heavenly beings gathered before God like courtiers before a king.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (18, 1, 13, 'Later in this passage, raiders strike Job''s herds. The Sabeans came from Sheba in Arabia, a people better known as traders; the Chaldeans were tribes from Mesopotamia. Here both appear as raiding bands.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (18, 2, 11, 'Eliphaz came from Teman in Edom, a place famed for its wise men. Bildad was a Shuhite and Zophar a Naamathite, peoples probably living in the Arabian or Edomite region.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (18, 28, 1, 'This poem describes ancient mining, with shafts cut into rock and tunnels lit in darkness, much like the copper and other mines worked in the lands south of the Dead Sea.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (18, 32, 1, 'Elihu is a Buzite, a name linked to Buz, a relative of Abraham. Some scholars think his speeches were added later, since he is not mentioned elsewhere in the book; others see them as part of the original design.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (18, 40, 15, 'Behemoth is often identified with the hippopotamus, though some see it as a legendary beast of great power. Later in this passage the description turns to Leviathan, often identified with the crocodile, a name also given to a sea monster in ancient Canaanite poems.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (18, 41, 12, 'This passage continues the long description of Leviathan, a creature often identified with the crocodile. The name also belongs to a fearsome sea monster in ancient Canaanite poems known to Israel''s neighbours.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 1, 1, 'Psalm 1 opens Book One of the five ''books'' Psalms is divided into, and many think it was placed first as a doorway into the whole collection.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 2, 1, 'Many scholars think this was sung at the crowning of a new king from David''s family in Jerusalem.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 3, 1, 'The heading links it to the time David fled Jerusalem when his son Absalom led a rebellion to seize the throne.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 7, 1, 'The heading links it to words of ''Cush, a Benjaminite'', someone from King Saul''s tribe, though nothing more is known of him.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 9, 1, 'Psalms 9 and 10 may once have been one poem: together they loosely follow the Hebrew alphabet, each section starting with the next letter.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 18, 1, 'The heading links it to the day God rescued David from Saul and all his enemies; a nearly identical song appears in 2 Samuel 22.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 20, 1, 'A prayer for the king, probably sung at the temple before he led the army out to battle.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 21, 1, 'A royal psalm, likely sung at the temple to give thanks for the king, perhaps after a victory.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 24, 1, 'Many think it was sung in procession as worshippers, perhaps carrying the ark of the covenant (Israel''s sacred chest), approached the temple gates.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 30, 1, 'The heading calls it a song for the dedication of ''the house'', probably the temple or David''s palace in Jerusalem.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 34, 1, 'The heading links it to when David, fleeing Saul, pretended to be mad before the Philistine king of Gath (called Abimelech here, Achish in 1 Samuel).');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 37, 1, 'This is an acrostic: each pair of verses begins with the next letter of the Hebrew alphabet, a pattern that may have helped people memorise it.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 41, 1, 'This closes Book One of Psalms; the final verse of praise was likely added to mark the end of that collection.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 42, 1, 'Opening Book Two, this is the first psalm of the ''sons of Korah'', a family of Levite temple singers; Psalms 42 and 43 were probably once one poem.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 45, 1, 'A song for a royal wedding, celebrating a king of Israel marrying a foreign princess; which king is unknown.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 50, 1, 'The first psalm credited to Asaph, a Levite whom David appointed to lead temple music; his family of singers carried on his name.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 51, 1, 'The heading links it to when the prophet Nathan confronted David after his adultery with Bathsheba and the arranged death of her husband Uriah.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 52, 1, 'The heading links it to when Doeg the Edomite told Saul that David had visited the priest Ahimelech, which led to the killing of the priests at Nob.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 53, 1, 'This is nearly the same as Psalm 14; it seems the song was copied into two different collections that were later joined together.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 54, 1, 'The heading links it to when people of Ziph, a town in Judah''s wilderness, betrayed David''s hiding place to King Saul.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 56, 1, 'The heading links it to when the Philistines seized David in Gath, Goliath''s hometown, where he had fled from Saul.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 57, 1, 'The heading links it to when David hid from Saul in a cave, either the cave of Adullam or one near En Gedi.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 59, 1, 'The heading links it to when Saul sent men to watch David''s house to kill him, and his wife Michal helped him escape.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 60, 1, 'The heading links it to David''s wars against the Arameans of Syria, when Joab''s army defeated the Edomites in the Valley of Salt.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 63, 1, 'The heading places David in the wilderness of Judah, the dry hill country west of the Dead Sea, fleeing either Saul or later Absalom.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 70, 1, 'This short psalm is almost identical to the closing verses of Psalm 40.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 72, 1, 'The heading credits it to Solomon, David''s son; a prayer for the king, it closes Book Two with a note that David''s prayers end here.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 73, 1, 'Opening Book Three, this begins a group of eleven psalms credited to Asaph and the temple singers who carried on his name.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 74, 1, 'It describes the temple burned by enemies, so most think it was written after the Babylonians destroyed Jerusalem in 586 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 79, 1, 'Like Psalm 74, it mourns Jerusalem in ruins and the temple defiled, most likely after the Babylonian conquest of 586 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 80, 1, 'Its mention of Ephraim, Benjamin and Manasseh leads many to link it to the northern kingdom''s collapse under Assyria around 722 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 81, 1, 'It was sung at a festival marked by trumpet blasts at the new and full moon, probably the autumn Feast of Tabernacles (Booths).');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 85, 1, 'Many think it comes from soon after the exiles returned from Babylon, when the land was poor and rebuilding was slow.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 88, 1, 'The heading credits it to the sons of Korah and to Heman the Ezrahite. A Heman led temple music in David''s day, and a famous wise man of that name is mentioned in Solomon''s time, though they may not be the same man.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 89, 1, 'Credited to Ethan the Ezrahite, it mourns a defeat of a king from David''s line, perhaps Jerusalem''s fall; it closes Book Three.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 90, 1, 'The heading credits it to Moses, making it traditionally the oldest psalm; it opens Book Four.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 92, 1, 'The heading calls it a song for the Sabbath day, the weekly day of rest, when it was sung at the temple.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 96, 1, 'Much of this psalm also appears in 1 Chronicles 16, sung when David brought the ark of the covenant (Israel''s sacred chest) to Jerusalem.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 100, 1, 'The heading calls it a psalm for giving thanks, probably sung as worshippers brought thank offerings into the temple courts.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 105, 1, 'Its opening verses also appear in 1 Chronicles 16, in the song sung when the ark was brought to Jerusalem.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 106, 1, 'It closes Book Four; its plea to be gathered from among the nations suggests it took shape when many Israelites were scattered in exile.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 107, 1, 'Opening Book Five, it speaks of people gathered from every direction, which many link to the return from exile in Babylon.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 108, 1, 'This psalm joins together parts of two earlier psalms, 57 and 60.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 110, 1, 'Credited to David, it is a royal psalm, probably used when a new king was enthroned in Jerusalem.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 113, 1, 'Psalms 113–118 form the ''Hallel'' (praise), sung by Jewish families at Passover; Jesus and his disciples probably sang them at the Last Supper.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 118, 1, 'The last of the Hallel psalms sung at Passover, it seems written for a thanksgiving procession entering the temple gates.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 119, 1, 'The longest psalm: 22 stanzas of eight verses, every verse in a stanza starting with the same letter, working through the Hebrew alphabet.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 120, 1, 'This opens the ''Songs of Ascents'' (120–134), probably sung by pilgrims as they went up to Jerusalem for the yearly festivals.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 122, 1, 'A pilgrim song credited to David, celebrating arrival at Jerusalem''s gates after the festival journey.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 126, 1, 'Most think it celebrates the return of exiles from Babylon after 538 BC, when the Persian king Cyrus allowed them home.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 127, 1, 'The only Song of Ascents credited to Solomon, the king who built the temple.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 132, 1, 'It recalls David''s resolve to find a home for the ark, Israel''s sacred chest, and may have been sung in temple processions.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 134, 1, 'The last Song of Ascents, addressed to the Levites who kept watch and served in the temple through the night.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 136, 1, 'Every line ends with the same refrain, likely sung responsively, with a leader singing and the congregation answering.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 137, 1, 'It comes from the Jews taken to Babylon after Jerusalem fell in 586 BC, recalling their time by Babylon''s rivers, whether written there or soon after returning home.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 142, 1, 'The heading links it to when David hid in a cave while fleeing King Saul.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 145, 1, 'The last psalm credited to David, an acrostic whose verses run through the Hebrew alphabet.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 146, 1, 'Psalms 146–150 form a closing group of praise, each beginning and ending with ''Hallelujah'', meaning ''Praise the LORD''.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 147, 1, 'Its mention of God rebuilding Jerusalem and gathering exiles suggests it comes from after the return from Babylon.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (19, 150, 1, 'The final psalm closes Book Five and the whole collection, naming many of the instruments used in temple worship.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (20, 1, 1, 'The opening title names Solomon, son of David, king of Israel, and begins a long section (chapters 1–9) of speeches from a parent or teacher to a young man.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (20, 10, 1, 'A fresh heading credits what follows to Solomon, starting the book''s main collection of short two-line sayings, which runs through to the middle of chapter 22.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (20, 22, 1, 'From verse 17 a new collection begins, credited to unnamed wise teachers; scholars widely note close parallels with an ancient Egyptian teaching text called the Instruction of Amenemope.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (20, 24, 1, 'The collection of sayings from unnamed wise teachers continues here, and verse 23 opens a short extra collection also credited to them.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (20, 25, 1, 'The heading says these Solomon sayings were copied out by the men of Hezekiah, king of Judah, around 700 BC, likely royal scribes at his court.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (20, 30, 1, 'These are credited to Agur son of Jakeh, otherwise unknown. The Hebrew word massa in the heading can be read as the name of a people in northern Arabia, suggesting he came from there, or as ''oracle'' (a solemn message), so translations differ.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (20, 31, 1, 'These words are credited to King Lemuel, otherwise unknown, as teaching his mother gave him; like Agur, he may have come from Massa in northern Arabia.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (20, 31, 10, 'This closing poem is an acrostic: in Hebrew, each verse begins with the next letter of the alphabet, a form that may have helped memory and gave a sense of completeness, from first letter to last.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (21, 2, 1, 'Later in this passage, the houses, vineyards, parks, pools, servants and singers the Teacher describes echo the famous wealth and building projects of Solomon''s court described in the books of Kings and Chronicles.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (21, 5, 1, '''The house of God'' is the temple in Jerusalem, where worshippers offered sacrifices and made vows, solemn promises to give God something.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (21, 8, 1, 'This section reflects life at a royal court, where advisers and officials served at the king''s pleasure and had to handle his commands carefully.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (21, 12, 9, 'These closing verses speak about the Teacher rather than in his voice. Many think they were added by a student or editor who gathered his words; others think the author himself wrote them as a closing summary.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (22, 1, 5, 'Kedar was a nomadic Arabian tribe known for tents woven from black goat hair, a familiar sight on the desert edges east of Israel.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (22, 1, 9, 'This passage opens by comparing the woman to a mare among Pharaoh''s chariots. Egypt''s pharaohs were famous for their fine chariot horses, which Solomon reportedly traded in.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (22, 3, 6, 'This section pictures King Solomon''s wedding procession arriving from the wilderness, with a royal carriage made of wood from Lebanon, famous for its cedars, and an armed guard.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (22, 4, 8, 'Lebanon and Amana, along with Senir and Hermon (two names for the same great mountain or range), are peaks in the far north, beyond Israel''s usual borders, where lions and leopards still roamed in ancient times.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (22, 6, 4, 'Tirzah was a beautiful city that served for a time as the capital of the northern kingdom of Israel, here placed alongside Jerusalem.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (22, 6, 11, 'Later in this passage, the poem names Heshbon, a town east of the Jordan known for its pools, along with Mount Carmel on the coast and the city of Damascus.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 1, 2, 'Many scholars link this picture of a land overrun and Jerusalem left isolated to the Assyrian invasion of Judah in 701 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 2, 1, 'An almost identical passage appears in Micah, a prophet working in Judah at the same time; scholars debate who borrowed from whom, or whether both drew on an older source.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 6, 1, 'This vision is set in the Jerusalem temple and dated to the year King Uzziah died, around 740 BC, after his long reign of about fifty years.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 7, 1, 'Around 735 BC the kings of Aram (Syria) and northern Israel marched on Jerusalem to force King Ahaz to join their alliance against Assyria.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 8, 1, 'This passage comes from the time around 735 BC when Aram (Syria) and northern Israel threatened Jerusalem; Assyria went on to capture Damascus in 732 BC and Samaria in 722 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 8, 16, 'Later in this passage, Zebulun and Naphtali are named; these areas, in Galilee in the far north, were among the first Assyria took from Israel, around 732 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 9, 8, 'This section is aimed at the northern kingdom of Israel, also called Ephraim, whose capital was Samaria, in the years before Assyria destroyed it in 722 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 10, 5, 'The cities listed, such as Carchemish, Hamath, Arpad and Damascus, all fell to Assyria''s armies during the decades of Isaiah''s ministry.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 10, 28, 'The towns named trace an army''s march from the north toward Jerusalem, ending at Nob, probably a hill within sight of the city.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 11, 1, 'Jesse was the father of King David, so this passage looks to David''s royal family, which still ruled Judah in Isaiah''s day.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 13, 1, 'This oracle concerns Babylon; some scholars place it in Isaiah''s day, when Babylon was resisting Assyria, while many date it later, when Babylon itself ruled the region.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 14, 28, 'This oracle is dated to the year King Ahaz died, around 727 or 715 BC (scholars differ on which), and addressed to Philistia, Judah''s neighbour on the Mediterranean coast.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 15, 1, 'Moab was a kingdom east of the Dead Sea, a close neighbour and frequent rival of Israel and Judah; the many towns named were Moabite cities.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 17, 1, 'Damascus was the capital of Aram, roughly modern Syria, which was allied with northern Israel against Judah; Assyria captured it in 732 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 18, 1, 'Cush was the land south of Egypt, roughly modern Sudan, whose kings ruled Egypt in this period and sent envoys seeking allies against Assyria.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 19, 1, 'Egypt was Judah''s powerful southern neighbour, and many in Jerusalem looked to it for protection against Assyria.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 20, 1, 'This is dated to around 711 BC, when Sargon of Assyria sent his commander to capture Ashdod, a Philistine city that had rebelled hoping for Egyptian help.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 21, 1, 'The ''wilderness of the sea'' refers to southern Babylonia; scholars debate whether this fall of Babylon happened in Isaiah''s lifetime or is Persia''s conquest of 539 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 21, 11, 'Dumah was an oasis in northern Arabia, and Seir is another name for Edom, the land south-east of the Dead Sea.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 21, 13, 'Dedan, Tema and Kedar were desert tribes and trading oases of northern Arabia, known for their camel caravans.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 22, 1, 'The ''Valley of Vision'' refers to Jerusalem; the strengthening of walls and storing of water are widely linked to Hezekiah''s preparations for the Assyrian siege around 701 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 22, 15, 'Shebna was the senior official in charge of the royal palace; an ancient tomb inscription found near Jerusalem may belong to him.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 23, 1, 'Tyre and Sidon were wealthy Phoenician port cities on the coast of modern Lebanon, famous for sea trade across the Mediterranean.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 24, 1, 'Chapters 24–27 are often called the ''Isaiah Apocalypse'' because, like the book of Revelation, they describe judgment and renewal on a worldwide scale; scholars debate their date, with suggestions ranging from Isaiah''s own time to well after the exile.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 28, 1, 'This is addressed first to Samaria, capital of the northern kingdom, before its fall to Assyria in 722 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 28, 14, 'The ''covenant with death'' is widely thought to refer to the Jerusalem leaders'' alliance with Egypt against Assyria during Hezekiah''s reign.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 29, 1, '''Ariel'' is a poetic name for Jerusalem, the city where King David had set up his camp.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 30, 1, 'Judah''s leaders were sending envoys to Egypt, to cities such as Zoan, seeking an alliance against Assyria, probably in the years before 701 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 34, 1, 'Edom, south-east of the Dead Sea, was Judah''s neighbour and long-standing rival; Bozrah was one of its chief cities.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 36, 1, 'This is dated to Hezekiah''s fourteenth year, 701 BC, when Sennacherib of Assyria invaded Judah; much the same account appears in 2 Kings 18–19.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 38, 9, 'The heading presents this poem as written by King Hezekiah himself after he recovered from his illness.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 39, 1, 'Merodach-baladan was a Babylonian leader who repeatedly rebelled against Assyria; his envoys were likely seeking Judah''s support.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 40, 1, 'Many scholars think chapters 40–66 were written for Jews exiled in Babylon around 550–540 BC; the traditional view is that Isaiah foresaw that time.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 41, 1, 'The conqueror stirred up from the east is widely identified as Cyrus, the Persian king who conquered much of the region in the 540s BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 42, 1, 'This is the first of four passages scholars call the ''Servant Songs'', which centre on a figure called the Lord''s servant.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 44, 24, 'Cyrus is named here: the Persian king who captured Babylon in 539 BC and allowed exiled Jews to return home.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 46, 1, 'Bel and Nebo were leading gods of Babylon, whose statues were carried through the city in great religious processions.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 47, 1, 'Babylon was famous for its astrologers and stargazers, whom its kings consulted to read the future.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 52, 13, 'This is the last and longest of four passages scholars call the "Servant Songs", which centre on a figure called the Lord''s servant.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 56, 1, 'Many scholars link chapters 56–66 to the years after exiles began returning to Jerusalem in 538 BC; others keep them with Isaiah''s own work.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 63, 1, 'Bozrah was a chief city of Edom, Judah''s south-eastern neighbour and old rival.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 63, 15, 'The burned temple and ruined cities described here are widely linked to Babylon''s destruction of Jerusalem in 587 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (23, 66, 1, 'Many scholars connect this passage to the period of rebuilding the temple, which was finished around 515 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 1, 1, 'Anathoth was a village of priests in the tribe of Benjamin''s land; Jeremiah''s call is dated to Josiah''s thirteenth year, around 627 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 3, 6, 'This message is dated to King Josiah''s reign, when the northern kingdom of Israel had already been conquered by Assyria about a century earlier.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 7, 1, 'This ''temple sermon'' is widely linked with chapter 26, early in Jehoiakim''s reign around 609 BC; Shiloh, where the tabernacle (the portable worship tent) once stood, had long since been destroyed.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 11, 18, 'The plotters were men from Jeremiah''s own hometown of Anathoth, possibly including members of his priestly family.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 13, 1, 'The place named ''Perath'' is usually taken as the distant Euphrates River, though some think it means Parah, a spring near Anathoth.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 14, 1, 'The heading links this passage to a severe drought in Judah; its exact date is not known.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 19, 1, 'The Valley of Ben Hinnom, just south of Jerusalem, contained Topheth, a site where children had been sacrificed to foreign gods.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 21, 1, 'Set around 588 BC, when King Zedekiah sent messengers to Jeremiah as Nebuchadnezzar''s Babylonian army besieged Jerusalem.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 22, 10, 'Shallum is another name for King Jehoahaz, Josiah''s son, who reigned three months in 609 BC before Pharaoh Necho took him captive to Egypt.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 22, 13, 'Aimed at King Jehoiakim, who reigned 609–598 BC and was known for building himself a lavish palace using forced, unpaid labour.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 22, 24, 'Coniah is King Jehoiachin, who reigned only three months before being taken to Babylon in 597 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 24, 1, 'Set just after 597 BC, when King Jehoiachin, Judah''s officials and skilled craftsmen had been deported to Babylon.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 25, 1, 'Dated to Jehoiakim''s fourth year, 605 BC, the year Nebuchadnezzar became king and Babylon defeated Egypt at Carchemish.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 26, 1, 'Dated to the start of Jehoiakim''s reign, around 609 BC; the elders recall Micah, who prophesied about a century earlier under King Hezekiah.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 27, 1, 'Around 594 BC, envoys from Edom, Moab, Ammon, Tyre and Sidon met in Jerusalem, apparently to plan rebellion against Babylon.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 28, 1, 'Dated to Zedekiah''s fourth year, around 593 BC, when hopes ran high that the exiles of 597 BC would soon return.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 29, 1, 'A letter Jeremiah sent to the exiles in Babylon, carried by royal envoys, a few years after the deportation of 597 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 30, 1, 'Chapters 30–33 are often called the ''Book of Consolation''; some think parts were first addressed to descendants of the long-conquered northern kingdom.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 31, 15, 'Ramah, a town north of Jerusalem, was later where captives were gathered before deportation; one old tradition placed Rachel''s tomb in Benjamin''s territory, not far from Ramah, though the better-known site is near Bethlehem.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 32, 1, 'Dated to Zedekiah''s tenth year, around 587 BC, during the final siege, while Jeremiah was confined in the palace guardhouse.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 34, 1, 'During the final siege, Lachish and Azekah were the last fortified towns in Judah still holding out besides Jerusalem.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 34, 8, 'During the siege, owners freed their Hebrew slaves, then took them back when the Babylonian army briefly withdrew to face Egypt.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 35, 1, 'The Rekabites were a clan who lived in tents and avoided wine; under Jehoiakim they took refuge in Jerusalem from invading armies.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 36, 1, 'Dated to Jehoiakim''s fourth year, 605 BC; the public reading followed in his fifth year, during a fast, in late 604 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 37, 1, 'Set around 588 BC, when an Egyptian army marched north and the Babylonians temporarily lifted their siege of Jerusalem.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 39, 1, 'Jerusalem''s walls were breached in the summer of 587 or 586 BC, after a siege of about eighteen months.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 40, 7, 'Gedaliah, from a respected family of officials, was appointed governor by Babylon and ruled from Mizpah, a town north of Jerusalem.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 43, 8, 'Tahpanhes was an Egyptian border fortress in the eastern Nile Delta, where the Jewish refugees, taking Jeremiah with them, settled.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 44, 1, 'Addressed to Jewish communities scattered across Egypt, from Migdol and Tahpanhes in the north to Memphis and Pathros, meaning Upper Egypt.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 45, 1, 'Dated to 605 BC, when Baruch was writing down Jeremiah''s words on the scroll described in chapter 36.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 46, 1, 'Chapters 46–51 contain messages about foreign nations; this one recalls Egypt''s defeat by Babylon at Carchemish, on the Euphrates, in 605 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 46, 13, 'Concerns Nebuchadnezzar''s expected invasion of Egypt; Babylonian records mention a campaign against Egypt around 568 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 47, 1, 'Addressed to the Philistines on the coast west of Judah, dated before an attack by Pharaoh on their city of Gaza.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 48, 1, 'Moab was a kingdom east of the Dead Sea, a long-time neighbour and frequent rival of Israel and Judah.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 49, 1, 'Ammon lay east of the Jordan River, around Rabbah, the site of modern Amman in Jordan.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 49, 7, 'Edom lay south-east of the Dead Sea; Teman and Bozrah were among its chief places.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 49, 23, 'Damascus was the capital of Aram, Israel''s northern neighbour, with Hamath and Arpad farther north in Syria.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 49, 28, 'Kedar was an Arab tribe of the desert east of Judah, known for tents and herding. Hazor here is not the famous northern city but probably refers to Arab settlements in the same region.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 49, 34, 'Elam was a kingdom east of Babylon, in what is now south-western Iran; this message is dated to early in Zedekiah''s reign.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 50, 1, 'Chapters 50–51 concern Babylon itself, the empire then dominating Judah; some scholars date parts to later in the exile.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 51, 59, 'Dated to Zedekiah''s fourth year, around 593 BC, when royal official Seraiah, Baruch''s brother, travelled to Babylon with the king.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 52, 1, 'This closing chapter closely matches the account in 2 Kings 24–25 and is generally thought to have been added by a later editor.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 52, 28, 'These deportation figures, smaller than those in 2 Kings, may count only adult men or a particular group.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (24, 52, 31, 'Around 561 BC, the new Babylonian king Evil-merodach (Amel-Marduk) released Jehoiachin after thirty-seven years in prison.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (25, 3, 1, 'Much of this poem speaks in the voice of a single suffering man, rather than of Jerusalem pictured as a grieving woman; tradition has often linked it to Jeremiah, who was beaten, imprisoned and lowered into a muddy cistern, an underground tank for storing rainwater.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (25, 4, 1, 'This chapter''s mention of the king''s capture is widely taken to refer to Zedekiah, caught fleeing Jerusalem, and its closing lines address Edom, a neighbour that welcomed Judah''s fall.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (25, 5, 1, 'This closing prayer, spoken by the whole community, is not an alphabet poem and seems to look back from a time when Judah was living under foreign rule.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (26, 1, 1, 'Dated to the fifth year of King Jehoiachin''s exile, about 593 BC, by the Kebar canal in Babylonia; the passage''s mention of a thirtieth year is often taken as Ezekiel''s age.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (26, 8, 1, 'Dated to about 592 BC, while the elders of the exiles sat in Ezekiel''s house in Babylon.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (26, 8, 5, 'Later in this passage, the god Tammuz is mourned; he was a Mesopotamian god whose death was ritually lamented each year.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (26, 14, 12, 'The Daniel named here may be the exile in Babylon or Danel, an ancient righteous king known from older Canaanite stories; scholars differ.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (26, 16, 1, 'Jerusalem had been a Canaanite city, held by a people called the Jebusites, until King David captured it around 1000 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (26, 17, 1, 'Set against King Zedekiah breaking his sworn loyalty to Babylon and seeking military help from Egypt.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (26, 19, 1, 'Widely linked to Judah''s last kings, including Jehoahaz, taken to Egypt in 609 BC, and a king later taken to Babylon.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (26, 20, 1, 'Dated to about 591 BC, when some elders of the exiles came to Ezekiel to ask for a word from the Lord.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (26, 21, 18, 'Rabbah was the capital of Ammon, a kingdom east of the Jordan River, on the site of modern Amman.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (26, 23, 1, 'Samaria was the capital of the northern kingdom of Israel, destroyed by the Assyrian empire around 722 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (26, 24, 1, 'Dated to the very day Babylon''s siege of Jerusalem began, around January 588 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (26, 25, 1, 'Ammon, Moab, Edom and Philistia were Judah''s neighbours, several of whom welcomed or profited from Jerusalem''s fall.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (26, 26, 1, 'Dated to about 587 BC; Tyre was a wealthy island trading city in modern Lebanon, besieged by Nebuchadnezzar for around thirteen years.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (26, 28, 1, 'Tyre''s king at this time is generally thought to have been Ithobaal III, who reigned during Babylon''s long siege.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (26, 28, 20, 'Sidon was a Phoenician port city a little north of Tyre, its neighbour and trading partner.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (26, 29, 1, 'Dated to about January 587 BC, during Jerusalem''s siege; Egypt''s Pharaoh, probably Hophra, was the ally Judah hoped would rescue it.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (26, 29, 17, 'Dated to about 571 BC, the latest date in the book, soon after Nebuchadnezzar''s long siege of Tyre ended.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (26, 30, 20, 'Dated to about 587 BC, soon after an Egyptian army failed to break Babylon''s siege of Jerusalem.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (26, 31, 1, 'Dated to about 587 BC, during Babylon''s siege of Jerusalem.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (26, 32, 1, 'Dated to about 585 BC, after news of Jerusalem''s fall had reached the exiles.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (26, 32, 17, 'Dated to the twelfth year of the exile, around 586 or 585 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (26, 33, 21, 'Dated to about January 585 BC, when a refugee reached the exiles in Babylon with news that Jerusalem had fallen.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (26, 35, 1, 'Mount Seir is the hill country of Edom, south of the Dead Sea; Edom was remembered for joining in Jerusalem''s downfall.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (26, 38, 1, 'Gog''s identity is uncertain; some link the name to Gyges, a king of Lydia in modern Turkey, while others see a symbolic figure.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (26, 40, 1, 'Dated to about 573 BC, which the passage counts as the fourteenth year after Jerusalem''s fall and the twenty-fifth year of the exile.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (26, 40, 5, 'A cubit was a measure of about half a metre; the cubit used for measuring here was a longer one, a handbreadth more than usual.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (26, 44, 15, 'Zadok was chief priest under King David and King Solomon; his descendants had led the priesthood in Jerusalem''s temple.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (27, 1, 1, 'The third year of King Jehoiakim of Judah is around 605 BC, when Nebuchadnezzar began to take control of the region; tradition places the first captives and Temple treasures being taken to Babylon at this time, though Babylonian records do not mention it.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (27, 2, 1, 'At verse 4 the book switches from Hebrew to Aramaic, the common language of the Babylonian and Persian empires, and stays in Aramaic through chapter 7.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (27, 4, 28, 'No Babylonian record mentions this illness; some scholars link the story to King Nabonidus, who spent about ten years away from Babylon at the desert oasis of Tema.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (27, 5, 1, 'Babylonian records show Belshazzar was the son of King Nabonidus and ruled Babylon on his behalf while his father was away at Tema.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (27, 5, 17, 'Babylon fell to Cyrus of Persia in October 539 BC; a Babylonian record says his forces took the city without a battle.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (27, 6, 1, 'Darius the Mede is not named outside Daniel; suggestions include Cyrus himself or a governor he appointed, while others see the figure as a literary portrait.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (27, 7, 1, 'The first year of Belshazzar is around 553 BC, so this vision comes before the feast and fall of Babylon told in chapter 5.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (27, 8, 1, 'Dated around 551 BC, this vision is set at Susa, an ancient city later used as a Persian royal capital; from here the book returns to Hebrew.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (27, 9, 1, 'The first year of Darius places this prayer around 539 BC, just after Babylon fell to the Persians.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (27, 10, 1, 'The third year of Cyrus is around 536 BC, shortly after the first group of exiles had been permitted to return to Jerusalem.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (27, 11, 5, 'Most interpreters link the kings of the south and north here with the Ptolemy rulers of Egypt and the Seleucid rulers of Syria, around 320–175 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (27, 11, 21, 'These verses are widely linked to Antiochus IV, who in 167 BC banned Jewish worship and set up a pagan altar in the Jerusalem Temple.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (27, 11, 40, 'At the start of this passage, the account of a king''s final campaign and death does not match known accounts of Antiochus IV''s death in 164 BC. Many scholars think the writer was working shortly before that death and expected it to come about this way; others, especially those holding the traditional view, think these verses look ahead to a figure still further in the future.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (28, 1, 1, 'The opening names kings of Judah from Uzziah to Hezekiah and Jeroboam II of Israel, placing Hosea''s work in the second half of the 700s BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (28, 1, 2, 'In this passage Hosea''s first son is named Jezreel, recalling the valley where King Jehu slaughtered the family of King Ahab about a century earlier; Jeroboam II belonged to Jehu''s family line.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (28, 5, 8, 'Many scholars link this to the war of about 734–732 BC, when Israel and Syria fought Judah and Assyria stepped in; Gibeah and Ramah were border towns.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (28, 8, 1, 'Later in this passage comes talk of buying Assyria''s favour, which many connect with King Menahem''s heavy payment of silver to the Assyrian king around 740 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (28, 9, 1, 'This may have been spoken during an autumn harvest festival, when crowds gathered at threshing floors and shrines to celebrate the grain and grape harvests.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (28, 10, 1, 'Later in this passage, ''Beth-aven'', meaning ''house of wickedness'', appears as Hosea''s mocking name for Bethel, home of one of the kingdom''s royal golden calf shrines.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (28, 10, 9, 'Near the end of this passage comes a mention of an attack by ''Shalman'' on Beth-arbel, an event known only here; scholars are unsure whether Shalman was an Assyrian or a Moabite king.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (28, 11, 12, 'In this passage, the mention of sending olive oil to Egypt while making a treaty with Assyria probably reflects King Hoshea''s double-dealing shortly before Samaria fell.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (28, 13, 9, 'This passage asks where Israel''s king has gone. Many date it to Samaria''s final years, possibly after Assyria took King Hoshea prisoner around 724 BC, leaving Israel without a king.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (28, 14, 1, 'This passage, and the book, ends with a verse written like a wise saying, and many scholars think an editor added it when the book was gathered for later readers.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (29, 1, 13, 'Priests offered grain and wine to God at the temple every morning and evening; the famine meant these daily offerings could no longer be made.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (29, 2, 1, 'At the start of this passage a trumpet is blown: the shofar, a ram''s horn sounded from Zion, Jerusalem''s temple hill, to raise an alarm or call people together.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (29, 2, 12, 'In this passage the people are called to a fast and a sacred assembly, a recognised public response to national disaster, and the priests are to stand between the temple porch and the altar to pray.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (29, 3, 1, 'Later in this passage the valley of Jehoshaphat is named. The name means "the LORD judges," and the place is not clearly located; later tradition linked it to the Kidron Valley beside Jerusalem.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (30, 1, 1, 'The book dates Amos''s preaching to two years before a famous earthquake. A major earthquake in Uzziah''s reign was remembered for generations, and archaeologists find signs of one around 760 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (30, 1, 3, 'Damascus was the capital of Aram (roughly modern Syria), which had fought Israel for years over Gilead, the farmland east of the Jordan River.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (30, 1, 6, 'Gaza was one of the main Philistine cities on the Mediterranean coast, south-west of Judah.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (30, 1, 9, 'Tyre was a wealthy Phoenician port on the coast north of Israel, famous for its sea trade.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (30, 1, 11, 'Edom lay south-east of the Dead Sea; its people were traditionally descended from Esau, Jacob''s brother, and had a long history of conflict with Israel.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (30, 1, 13, 'Ammon lay east of the Jordan, around today''s Amman, and competed with Israel for control of Gilead.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (30, 2, 1, 'Moab was the kingdom east of the Dead Sea, a frequent neighbour and rival of both Israel and Edom.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (30, 2, 4, 'Judah was the southern kingdom, centred on Jerusalem, and Amos''s own homeland.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (30, 2, 6, 'Here Amos turns to the northern kingdom of Israel itself, the very people he had come to address.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (30, 3, 9, 'Samaria, Israel''s hilltop capital, had grand houses; archaeologists there have found carved ivory panels like the ivory-decorated homes mentioned in this passage.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (30, 4, 1, 'Bashan was rich pastureland east of the Jordan known for well-fed cattle; the words are aimed at wealthy women of Samaria.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (30, 4, 4, 'Bethel and Gilgal were the best-known worship sites in northern Israel; Bethel had been a royal shrine since the kingdom split around 930 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (30, 5, 4, 'This passage names Beersheba, a pilgrimage site in the far south of Judah, alongside Bethel and Gilgal. People from the northern kingdom travelled there to worship as well as going to Bethel and Gilgal.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (30, 5, 18, 'People in Amos''s day looked forward to ''the day of the Lord'', a time they expected God to step in and defeat Israel''s enemies.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (30, 6, 1, 'This passage names Calneh and Hamath in Syria and the Philistine city of Gath, cities that had suffered defeat. Some scholars think this refers to setbacks before Amos''s time; others think it refers to Assyrian conquests a few decades later, which would mean these lines were added after Amos.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (30, 6, 8, 'Later in this passage, Lo-debar and Karnaim are named; they were towns east of the Jordan captured in Jeroboam II''s campaigns, which had pushed Israel''s borders north toward Hamath.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (30, 7, 1, 'The ''king''s mowing'' probably refers to the first cut of hay taken for the royal herds, before the farmers'' own spring crop grew.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (30, 8, 4, 'Trade stopped on the new moon festival and the Sabbath; merchants measured grain by the ephah, a dry measure, and weighed silver in shekels.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (30, 8, 9, 'A major eclipse of the sun in 763 BC, total in Assyria and seen as a partial eclipse in Israel, may have been fresh in people''s memories, some scholars think.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (30, 8, 11, 'Later in this passage, Dan is named; it lay in the far north and was another royal shrine, set up alongside Bethel when the kingdom divided.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (30, 9, 1, 'The altar in this vision is usually understood to be the one at Bethel, the shrine where Amos had preached.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (30, 9, 7, 'Cush was the land south of Egypt; Caphtor is usually identified with Crete, the Philistines'' homeland; Kir was the Arameans'' place of origin.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (30, 9, 11, 'Many scholars think this hopeful ending was added after Jerusalem fell in 586 BC; others hold that it comes from Amos himself.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (31, 1, 1, 'Edom''s homeland lay in the rugged red-rock mountains south-east of the Dead Sea, where cliff-top settlements made its people feel safe from attack. This passage closely parallels Jeremiah 49.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (31, 1, 5, 'Later in this passage Teman is mentioned. It was a region of Edom, and Edom was famous in the ancient world for its wise men and advisers.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (31, 1, 10, 'This section is widely linked to Jerusalem''s fall in 586 BC; Psalm 137 also remembers Edomites cheering the city''s destruction.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (31, 1, 17, 'Later in this passage a list of places is named, spanning Israel''s old lands and beyond. One of them, Sepharad, is uncertain but often identified with Sardis, a city in what is now western Turkey.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (32, 1, 1, 'Two places come up later in this passage: Joppa was a port on Israel''s Mediterranean coast, and Tarshish was a distant trading place far to the west, often linked with southern Spain, the opposite direction from Nineveh.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (32, 1, 17, 'Later in this passage, Jonah''s prayer is written as a poem in the style of the thanksgiving psalms that Israelites sang at the temple in Jerusalem.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (33, 1, 2, 'This opening calls all peoples to listen, with God himself as witness against them, then turns to Samaria, capital of the northern kingdom, and to Jerusalem. Assyria besieged and destroyed Samaria in 722 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (33, 1, 8, 'This lament names towns in Micah''s home region, including Lachish and Moresheth, many with wordplay on their names; it is often linked to Assyria''s advance through Judah.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (33, 3, 9, 'About a century later, Jeremiah 26 records elders recalling this warning about Jerusalem, saying King Hezekiah responded by turning to God.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (33, 4, 1, 'A nearly identical passage appears in Isaiah 2; scholars debate whether one prophet borrowed from the other or both drew on an older saying.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (33, 4, 9, 'This section mentions Babylon, which became the great power a century later; some scholars date it after the exile, while others see it as Micah''s prediction.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (33, 5, 2, 'Bethlehem Ephrathah was the small hometown of King David''s family; centuries later Matthew''s Gospel recalls this passage in telling of Jesus'' birth.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (33, 6, 1, 'Written like a courtroom case, it recalls Moses, Aaron and Miriam, Balak king of Moab, and Israel''s journey from Shittim across the Jordan to Gilgal.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (33, 6, 6, 'Later in this passage the speaker wonders about offering even a firstborn child. Child sacrifice was practised among neighbouring peoples, and the Bible records that King Ahaz of Judah, in Micah''s own day, sacrificed his son.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (33, 6, 9, 'Omri and Ahab were northern Israelite kings over a century earlier; Ahab was remembered for Baal worship and for seizing Naboth''s vineyard.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (33, 7, 14, 'Bashan and Gilead were rich grazing lands east of the Jordan, taken from Israel by Assyria around 733 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (34, 1, 1, 'Nineveh, the subject of the whole book, was Assyria''s capital, a vast walled city on the Tigris River near modern Mosul in northern Iraq.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (34, 2, 3, 'Nineveh fell in 612 BC to an alliance of Babylonians and Medes, who joined forces against the weakening Assyrian empire.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (34, 2, 11, 'Assyrian kings prized the lion as a royal symbol, hunting lions for sport and carving lion hunts on the walls of their palaces at Nineveh.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (34, 3, 8, 'Thebes, called No-Amon here, was Egypt''s great city on the Nile, sacked by the Assyrian king Ashurbanipal around 663 BC despite its powerful African allies.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (35, 1, 5, 'The Chaldeans named in this passage are the Babylonians. The name comes from the Chaldean peoples of southern Mesopotamia, modern Iraq, and became a common name for Babylon''s rulers and armies.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (35, 2, 6, 'This begins a series of five ''woe'' sayings, shaped like a mocking song imagined on the lips of the nations a conqueror had plundered.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (35, 3, 1, 'The heading calls this a prayer set to ''Shigionoth'', a musical term whose exact meaning is now uncertain, suggesting it was sung in worship.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (35, 3, 3, 'Teman and Mount Paran were places in the wild southern lands toward Edom and Sinai, the region older Israelite songs linked with God''s coming.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (35, 3, 16, 'At the end of this passage, the prayer closes with an instruction to the choir leader to accompany it on stringed instruments, like many headings in the Psalms.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (36, 1, 2, 'The practices named in this passage, Baal worship, bowing to the stars on rooftops and honouring Milcom, the god of the neighbouring Ammonites, had taken root in Judah, especially under Manasseh, and were later removed by Josiah.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (36, 1, 7, 'Later in this passage, the Fish Gate was one of Jerusalem''s city gates, while the Second Quarter and the Mortar were districts of the city, the last probably a hollow where merchants and traders did business.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (36, 2, 4, 'Gaza, Ashkelon, Ashdod and Ekron were Philistine cities on the coastal plain west of Judah, long-standing rivals of the Israelites.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (36, 2, 8, 'Moab and Ammon were neighbouring peoples east of the Jordan River and the Dead Sea, with a long history of hostility toward Israel and Judah.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (36, 2, 12, 'Cush was the land along the upper Nile, south of Egypt; Assyria''s capital Nineveh was the greatest city of the day, destroyed by Babylonians and Medes in 612 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (36, 3, 14, 'Traditionally this closing song is read as Zephaniah''s own; some scholars think it was added later, perhaps after the exile, when many people of Judah were taken to Babylon following Jerusalem''s fall around 586 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (37, 1, 1, 'Dated to the first day of the sixth month in Darius''s second year, around late August 520 BC, and addressed to Zerubbabel the governor and Joshua the high priest.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (37, 1, 12, 'Work on the temple resumed on the twenty-fourth day of that same month, around September 520 BC, just over three weeks after Haggai''s first message.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (37, 2, 1, 'Dated to the twenty-first day of the seventh month, around October 520 BC, the last day of the Feast of Tabernacles, a week-long harvest festival when people lived in shelters.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (37, 2, 10, 'Dated to the twenty-fourth day of the ninth month, around December 520 BC, three months after rebuilding resumed, and includes questions put to the priests about rules of ritual cleanness, that is, whether a person or thing counted as fit to take part in worship.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (37, 2, 20, 'Given on the same December day as the previous message and addressed to Zerubbabel alone, a grandson of King Jehoiachin and so a descendant of King David.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (38, 1, 1, 'Dated to the eighth month of Darius''s second year, around October or November 520 BC, shortly after Haggai began urging the people to rebuild.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (38, 1, 7, 'Dated to the twenty-fourth day of the eleventh month, around February 519 BC; this opens a series of eight visions Zechariah saw in a single night.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (38, 2, 6, 'Part of this section is addressed to Jews who were still living in Babylon rather than returning to Jerusalem.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (38, 3, 1, 'Joshua was the high priest, the chief religious leader of the returned community and the first to hold that office after the exile.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (38, 4, 1, 'Zerubbabel was the Persian-appointed governor of Judah, a descendant of King David, and he led the work of rebuilding the temple.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (38, 6, 9, 'Three men, Heldai, Tobijah and Jedaiah, had just arrived from the exiles in Babylon bringing gifts of silver and gold.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (38, 7, 1, 'Dated to late 518 BC, when a delegation, probably sent from the town of Bethel, asked whether the people should keep fasting to mourn the temple''s destruction by Babylon in 586 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (38, 8, 18, 'The four fasts mentioned recalled sad events around Jerusalem''s fall: the siege, the city walls breached, the temple burned, and the murder of governor Gedaliah.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (38, 9, 1, 'Names cities of Syria, Phoenicia and Philistia; some scholars connect this with Alexander the Great''s march down that coast in 332 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (38, 9, 11, 'This passage mentions Greece, one reason some scholars date these later chapters to a time when Greek power was rising.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (38, 12, 10, 'Later in this passage there is a reference to mourning at Hadad-rimmon near Megiddo, often linked to the grief after good King Josiah was killed in battle there in 609 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (38, 14, 1, 'Later in this passage there is a reference to an earthquake in the days of King Uzziah of Judah, around 760 BC, which Israel remembered for centuries afterward.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (38, 14, 16, 'The Feast of Tabernacles, or Booths, was an autumn harvest festival when Israelites lived in temporary shelters, remembering their wilderness journey after leaving Egypt.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (39, 1, 2, 'Edom, the neighbouring nation descended from Esau, had gloated over Jerusalem''s fall in 586 BC and was itself being overrun and pushed from its homeland around this time.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (39, 1, 6, 'The governor mentioned later in this passage was the official appointed by the Persian king to oversee Judah, which is one reason the book is placed in the Persian period.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (39, 2, 10, 'Marriages to women who worshipped other gods were a problem Ezra and Nehemiah also confronted in Jerusalem during this era. This passage also speaks of men divorcing their wives.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (39, 3, 6, 'Later in this passage the people are faulted over tithes, a tenth of their produce. Nehemiah''s records describe the same problem: people stopped bringing tithes, so temple storerooms stood empty and Levites left to farm their fields.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (39, 4, 4, 'Horeb is another name for Mount Sinai. These closing lines end the collection known as the Twelve Prophets (often called the Minor Prophets, Hosea to Malachi) and, in most Christian Bibles, the whole Old Testament.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (40, 2, 1, 'Herod the Great, Rome''s appointed king of Judea, died around 4 BC. The magi were likely learned star-watchers from the east, perhaps Persia or Babylon.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (40, 2, 13, 'Egypt, a Roman province outside Herod''s kingdom, had large Jewish communities, especially in the city of Alexandria. Later in this passage, after Herod died, his son Archelaus ruled Judea so harshly that Rome removed him in AD 6; Galilee, where Nazareth lay, went to another son, Herod Antipas.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (40, 3, 1, 'John baptised in the Jordan River near the Judean desert, probably around AD 28. Pharisees were strict keepers of Jewish law; Sadducees were the wealthy priestly party.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (40, 4, 12, 'Capernaum was a busy fishing town on the north shore of the Sea of Galilee, in the territory of Herod Antipas, who had arrested John.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (40, 4, 23, 'This passage moves from Jesus travelling Galilee''s towns, teaching in their synagogues (local meeting houses where Jews gathered to pray and hear the Scriptures read), to the start of his teaching on a hillside. That hillside is traditionally placed above the northwest shore of the Sea of Galilee, near Capernaum.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (40, 5, 38, 'Under Roman rule, soldiers could force civilians to carry their gear for a distance.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (40, 8, 5, 'A centurion was an army officer over about a hundred men, probably a non-Jew serving Herod Antipas or Rome at Capernaum.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (40, 8, 28, 'The region of the Gadarenes lay on the southeast side of the Sea of Galilee, a largely non-Jewish area, which is why pigs were kept there.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (40, 9, 9, 'This passage opens with the call of the man tradition names as the gospel''s author, sitting at a toll booth, probably in Capernaum.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (40, 11, 20, 'Chorazin, Bethsaida and Capernaum were Jewish towns near the north end of the lake; Tyre and Sidon were wealthy non-Jewish ports on the Mediterranean coast.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (40, 14, 1, 'Herod Antipas ruled Galilee for Rome. The Jewish historian Josephus says John was held at Machaerus, a fortress east of the Dead Sea.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (40, 15, 21, 'Tyre and Sidon were non-Jewish coastal cities in what is now Lebanon, northwest of Galilee.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (40, 16, 13, 'Caesarea Philippi lay near the foot of Mount Hermon, by a spring with a shrine to the Greek god Pan; Herod''s son Philip renamed it honouring Caesar.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (40, 17, 1, 'The mountain is not named; it is traditionally identified as Mount Tabor in Galilee, though some suggest Mount Hermon.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (40, 17, 22, 'Later in this passage, collectors ask about the temple tax, a yearly payment of two drachmas, about two days'' wages, expected of Jewish men to support the Jerusalem temple.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (40, 19, 1, 'Jewish teachers of the day were divided on divorce: one school allowed it for many reasons, another, stricter school mainly for unfaithfulness.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (40, 20, 29, 'Jericho was the last town on the pilgrim road before the steep climb of about fifteen miles up to Jerusalem.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (40, 21, 1, 'Jerusalem was crowded with pilgrims arriving for Passover. Bethphage and the Mount of Olives lay just east of the city.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (40, 21, 12, 'In the temple''s outer court, traders sold animals for sacrifice and money changers swapped pilgrims'' coins for the special currency used for temple payments.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (40, 22, 15, 'The Herodians supported Herod''s family. The tax in question was paid to Rome in silver coins bearing the emperor''s image, and was deeply resented.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (40, 22, 23, 'The Sadducees, the priestly upper class, gave the five books of Moses chief authority (some think they accepted only these) and did not believe in resurrection.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (40, 24, 1, 'Herod the Great had rebuilt and greatly enlarged the temple; work was still continuing in Jesus'' day. The Romans destroyed it in AD 70.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (40, 26, 6, 'Bethany was a village about two miles east of Jerusalem, on the far side of the Mount of Olives.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (40, 26, 17, 'Passover was the yearly meal remembering Israel''s escape from Egypt, eaten in Jerusalem with lamb, unleavened bread and wine.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (40, 26, 36, 'Gethsemane, meaning "oil press", was an olive grove on the Mount of Olives, just across the Kidron Valley from the city walls.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (40, 26, 57, 'Caiaphas was high priest from about AD 18 to 36. The council, called the Sanhedrin, was the Jewish ruling body in Jerusalem under Rome.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (40, 27, 11, 'Pontius Pilate was Roman governor of Judea from AD 26 to 36, in Jerusalem for Passover to keep order; Rome generally kept the power of execution in its own hands.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (40, 27, 32, 'Crucifixion took place outside the city walls, probably around AD 30 or 33. Cyrene was a city in North Africa, in present-day Libya.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (40, 27, 57, 'Arimathea was a Judean town. Wealthy families had tombs cut into rock, sealed with a large stone rolled across the entrance.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (41, 1, 21, 'Capernaum, a fishing town on the north shore of the Sea of Galilee, became Jesus'' base; a synagogue was the local Jewish meeting place for prayer and Scripture reading.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (41, 2, 13, 'Tax collectors gathered tolls for Herod Antipas and Rome, often taking extra for themselves, so fellow Jews widely despised them as cheats and collaborators.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (41, 2, 23, 'The Sabbath, from Friday sunset to Saturday sunset, was the weekly day of rest; Pharisees, a respected group devoted to strict law-keeping, counted plucking grain as forbidden harvesting work.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (41, 3, 1, 'The Herodians were supporters of Herod Antipas and his family, normally unlikely allies for the Pharisees.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (41, 5, 1, 'This took place on the lake''s eastern side, near the Decapolis, a region of Greek-style cities where most people were not Jewish, which explains the herd of pigs.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (41, 6, 14, 'This ''King Herod'' was Herod Antipas, ruler of Galilee; the Jewish historian Josephus also records that he imprisoned and executed John the Baptist, at his fortress of Machaerus.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (41, 7, 24, 'Tyre was an ancient non-Jewish port city on the Mediterranean coast north of Galilee, in what is now Lebanon.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (41, 8, 27, 'Caesarea Philippi lay far north near Mount Hermon, a city rebuilt by Herod''s son Philip, named after the emperor, and home to a shrine to the Greek god Pan.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (41, 9, 2, 'The mountain is not named; tradition identifies it as Mount Tabor in Galilee, though some suggest Mount Hermon near Caesarea Philippi.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (41, 10, 1, 'The region across the Jordan, called Perea, was also ruled by Herod Antipas, and divorce was a hotly debated question among Jewish teachers of the day.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (41, 10, 46, 'Jericho was the last town on the steep road up to Jerusalem, about fifteen miles away, and crowds of pilgrims passed through on their way to Passover.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (41, 11, 1, 'Bethphage and Bethany were villages on the Mount of Olives, just east of Jerusalem, where the city was filling with pilgrims for Passover.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (41, 11, 15, 'In the temple''s large outer court, traders sold animals for sacrifice and money changers swapped Roman coins for the special coinage required for the temple tax.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (41, 12, 13, 'The tax was a Roman tribute paid in silver denarius coins stamped with the emperor Tiberius'' image, a sore point for many Jews.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (41, 12, 18, 'The Sadducees were a wealthy priestly group who rejected the Pharisees'' unwritten traditions and did not believe in resurrection; some ancient writers say they accepted only the first five books of Scripture, though many scholars doubt this.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (41, 12, 41, 'The temple treasury probably refers to offering chests, said to be shaped like trumpets, that stood in the temple''s Court of the Women.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (41, 13, 1, 'Herod the Great began rebuilding the temple around 20 BC with enormous white stones, and work was still continuing in Jesus'' day.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (41, 14, 1, 'Passover, followed by the week-long Feast of Unleavened Bread, recalled Israel''s escape from Egypt and drew huge crowds to Jerusalem each spring.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (41, 14, 12, 'Passover lambs were sacrificed at the temple on the afternoon before the Passover meal, and the meal had to be eaten within Jerusalem after sunset.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (41, 14, 32, 'Gethsemane, meaning ''oil press'', was an olive grove on the slope of the Mount of Olives, across the Kidron Valley from the city.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (41, 14, 43, 'Later in this passage a young man runs away. Some have long suggested he was Mark himself, though the Gospel never says so.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (41, 14, 53, 'The council, often called the Sanhedrin, was the Jewish ruling body led by the high priest, who at this time was Caiaphas.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (41, 15, 1, 'Pontius Pilate, Roman governor of Judea from about AD 26 to 36, usually lived on the coast but came to Jerusalem at Passover to keep order.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (41, 15, 21, 'Cyrene was a city in North Africa with a large Jewish community; crucifixion was a public Roman execution reserved for rebels, slaves and serious criminals.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (41, 16, 9, 'The oldest surviving manuscripts end at verse 8; most scholars think these closing verses were added later, though some defend them and the church has long read them.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (42, 1, 1, 'This formal opening follows the style Greek historians and writers of the day used when dedicating a work to a patron.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (42, 1, 5, 'Herod the Great ruled Judea for Rome until about 4 BC; priests served at the temple in rotating groups, and offering incense was a rare, lot-chosen honour.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (42, 2, 1, 'Augustus was Rome''s first emperor; scholars debate how this census fits with Quirinius''s known census of AD 6 and Herod''s death around 4 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (42, 2, 21, 'Jewish law required a mother''s purification offering about forty days after a birth; the two-bird offering was the option allowed for poorer families.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (42, 2, 41, 'Jewish families travelled to Jerusalem each spring for Passover; at around twelve a boy was nearing the age of taking on adult religious duties.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (42, 3, 1, 'The fifteenth year of Tiberius is around AD 28–29; Pontius Pilate governed Judea about AD 26–36, while Herod Antipas ruled Galilee.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (42, 4, 14, 'In a synagogue Sabbath service, scripture was read aloud from scrolls, and a visiting teacher could be invited to read and speak.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (42, 4, 31, 'Capernaum was a fishing town on the north shore of the Sea of Galilee, and it became Jesus'' base during his work in Galilee.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (42, 5, 1, 'The Lake of Gennesaret is another name for the Sea of Galilee, a freshwater lake whose fishing trade supported the towns around its shore.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (42, 5, 17, 'Pharisees were a respected lay movement devoted to strict keeping of God''s law; teachers of the law were experts who studied and explained it.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (42, 5, 27, 'Tax collectors gathered tolls and taxes for Herod Antipas or Rome, often overcharging, so they were widely despised as cheats and collaborators.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (42, 7, 1, 'A centurion was a Gentile officer commanding around a hundred soldiers, here probably serving Herod Antipas in Capernaum.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (42, 7, 11, 'Nain was a small village in southern Galilee, a few miles from Nazareth.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (42, 8, 26, 'The region of the Gerasenes lay across the lake in largely Gentile territory, which explains the herd of pigs, animals Jews did not keep.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (42, 9, 7, 'Herod Antipas, son of Herod the Great, ruled Galilee and had imprisoned and executed John the Baptist.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (42, 9, 10, 'Bethsaida was a town near where the Jordan River enters the north end of the Sea of Galilee.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (42, 9, 51, 'Samaritans lived between Galilee and Judea; they and Jews had centuries of bitter disputes over worship, and pilgrims to Jerusalem were often unwelcome.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (42, 10, 25, 'The road from Jerusalem down to Jericho dropped steeply through lonely desert for about seventeen miles and was notorious for robbers.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (42, 10, 38, 'John''s Gospel places the home of Martha and Mary in Bethany, a village just outside Jerusalem.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (42, 13, 1, 'This killing of Galileans by Pilate is not recorded elsewhere; the tower of Siloam stood near the Pool of Siloam in Jerusalem.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (42, 17, 11, 'People with skin diseases were required by law to live apart from others and keep their distance, often in small outcast groups.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (42, 18, 35, 'Jericho was a lush oasis city about fifteen miles northeast of Jerusalem as the crow flies (a little farther by the winding road), a usual stop for pilgrims on the way up to the city.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (42, 19, 1, 'Jericho sat on a busy trade route, making it an important tax post where a chief tax collector could grow very rich.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (42, 19, 11, 'Listeners may have recalled Herod''s son Archelaus, who travelled to Rome around 4 BC to be made king while a Jewish delegation opposed him.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (42, 19, 28, 'Bethphage and Bethany were villages on the Mount of Olives, the ridge facing Jerusalem from the east across a valley.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (42, 19, 45, 'In the temple''s outer court, traders sold animals for sacrifice and changed money into the coins required for the temple tax.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (42, 20, 20, 'Paying the Roman tax was a bitterly divisive question; the denarius, a silver coin, carried the emperor''s image and title.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (42, 20, 27, 'The Sadducees were a wealthy priestly party linked with temple leadership who did not believe in a resurrection of the dead.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (42, 21, 5, 'Herod the Great began rebuilding the temple on a grand scale around 20 BC; the Romans destroyed it in AD 70.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (42, 21, 20, 'Roman armies besieged and destroyed Jerusalem in AD 70; scholars differ on whether Luke wrote before or after this event.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (42, 22, 1, 'Passover recalled Israel''s escape from Egypt and ran into the week-long Festival of Unleavened Bread, when Jerusalem filled with pilgrims.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (42, 22, 63, 'The council, often called the Sanhedrin, was the Jewish ruling body of priests and elders, led by the high priest.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (42, 23, 1, 'Only the Roman governor could order an execution; Herod Antipas was in Jerusalem for Passover, and only Luke records his part in the trial.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (42, 23, 26, 'Cyrene was a city in North Africa, in modern Libya, with a large Jewish community whose members came to Jerusalem for festivals.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (42, 23, 32, 'Crucifixion was a public Roman execution used mainly for slaves and rebels, carried out outside Jerusalem''s walls at a place called The Skull.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (42, 23, 50, 'Wealthy families used tombs cut into rock, sealed with a stone; burial had to be hurried because the Sabbath began at sunset on Friday.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (42, 24, 13, 'The exact site of Emmaus is uncertain; it lay about seven miles from Jerusalem.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (42, 24, 50, 'Bethany lay on the eastern slope of the Mount of Olives, about two miles from Jerusalem.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (43, 1, 1, 'This opening, often called the prologue, uses ''the Word'' (Greek logos), a term familiar both to Jewish readers and to Greek thinkers of the day.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (43, 2, 1, 'Village weddings could last several days, and the host family was expected to keep guests supplied; running out of wine would have been a real embarrassment.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (43, 2, 12, 'Passover was the spring festival remembering Israel''s escape from Egypt, drawing huge crowds of pilgrims to Jerusalem. Here John places the temple clearing early; the other Gospels place it near the end.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (43, 3, 1, 'Pharisees were a respected Jewish group devoted to careful keeping of God''s law; Nicodemus also sat on the ruling council in Jerusalem.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (43, 4, 1, 'Jews and Samaritans had been bitterly divided for centuries over worship and ancestry; Samaritans worshipped on Mount Gerizim, near Jacob''s well, rather than in Jerusalem.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (43, 4, 46, 'The royal official was most likely in the service of Herod Antipas, the ruler of Galilee under Rome.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (43, 5, 1, 'Archaeologists have uncovered a large pool complex near Jerusalem''s Sheep Gate, widely identified as Bethesda, matching the description of pools surrounded by covered walkways.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (43, 6, 1, 'The Sea of Galilee was also called the Sea of Tiberias, after the city Herod Antipas built on its shore in honour of the Emperor Tiberius.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (43, 7, 1, 'The Festival of Tabernacles, or Booths, was an autumn harvest celebration when families lived in temporary shelters, remembering Israel''s years in the wilderness.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (43, 7, 37, 'Later Jewish writings describe priests carrying water from the Pool of Siloam each day of this festival and pouring it out at the temple altar.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (43, 7, 53, 'This story does not appear in the earliest surviving manuscripts of John, and many Bibles mark it off; it was likely an early account added later.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (43, 8, 12, 'During Tabernacles, great lamps were lit in the temple courts, reportedly lighting up much of Jerusalem; the treasury was in this same area.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (43, 8, 48, 'Later in this passage, the Pool of Siloam is mentioned. It lay in the southern part of Jerusalem, and its remains were rediscovered by archaeologists in 2004.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (43, 10, 22, 'The Festival of Dedication, now called Hanukkah, remembered the temple''s rededication in 164 BC after Jewish rebels freed it from a foreign king who had defiled it.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (43, 11, 45, 'Caiaphas served as high priest from about AD 18 to 36, holding office with Roman approval; the council was the Sanhedrin, Jerusalem''s highest Jewish court.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (43, 12, 12, 'Palm branches had been a symbol of Jewish national hope and victory since the Jewish revolt against foreign rule in the 160s BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (43, 12, 20, 'These Greeks were probably non-Jews who worshipped Israel''s God and had come to Jerusalem for the Passover festival.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (43, 13, 1, 'Washing guests'' dusty feet was normally a servant''s task. John sets this meal just before Passover, a timing that differs slightly from the other Gospels.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (43, 18, 1, 'The garden lay across the Kidron Valley on the Mount of Olives; Matthew and Mark call it Gethsemane.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (43, 18, 12, 'Annas had been high priest around AD 6 to 15 and, as Caiaphas''s father-in-law, remained a powerful figure in the priestly family.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (43, 18, 28, 'Pontius Pilate governed Judea for Rome from about AD 26 to 36; Rome generally kept the power to carry out executions in the governor''s hands, though historians debate how strictly this applied.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (43, 19, 17, 'Crucifixion was a Roman punishment for slaves, rebels and criminals, carried out publicly outside city walls as a warning to others.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (43, 19, 38, 'Wealthy families buried their dead in tombs cut into rock, wrapping the body in linen with spices; the amount described here was lavish.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (43, 21, 1, 'Traditionally this final chapter has been read as part of the author''s own work; many scholars think it was added as an epilogue, possibly by the author''s circle, after the book''s first ending.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (43, 21, 20, 'The closing verses are partly written as ''we''. Traditionally they have been read as the author''s own words; many scholars think his community added them, possibly after the beloved disciple''s death, to address a rumour about him.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (44, 2, 1, 'Pentecost was a Jewish harvest festival held fifty days after Passover, when pilgrims from across the Jewish world crowded into Jerusalem.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (44, 3, 1, 'Three in the afternoon was a set time of daily prayer at the temple, matching the evening sacrifice, so the courts would have been busy.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (44, 3, 11, 'Solomon''s Colonnade was a long covered walkway along the temple''s east side; the Sadducees, a priestly party controlling the temple, rejected belief in resurrection.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (44, 5, 33, 'Gamaliel was a famous teacher among the Pharisees, a respected group devoted to careful keeping of God''s law; Paul later says he trained under him.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (44, 6, 1, 'The ''Hellenists'' were Jews raised abroad who spoke Greek, while the ''Hebrews'' were local Jews speaking Aramaic, the everyday language of Judea.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (44, 8, 4, 'Samaritans lived between Judea and Galilee, sharing ancestry and the books of Moses with Jews, but the two groups had been bitterly divided for centuries.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (44, 8, 26, '''Ethiopia'' here means the kingdom of Kush, south of Egypt in today''s Sudan; ''Candace'' was a royal title for its queen, not a personal name.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (44, 10, 1, 'Caesarea was a grand port city on the coast, built by Herod the Great and serving as the seat of the Roman governor of Judea.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (44, 11, 19, 'Antioch in Syria was among the empire''s largest cities; a serious famine struck Judea in the mid-40s AD during the reign of Emperor Claudius.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (44, 12, 1, 'This Herod is Herod Agrippa I, grandson of Herod the Great, who ruled Judea as king for Rome from about AD 41 to 44.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (44, 12, 20, 'The Jewish historian Josephus also records Herod Agrippa I''s sudden death at Caesarea in AD 44, during public festivities.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (44, 13, 13, 'Pisidian Antioch was a Roman colony in the highlands of what is now central Turkey, a different city from Antioch in Syria.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (44, 14, 8, 'A local legend told of the gods Zeus and Hermes once visiting this region in human disguise, which may explain the crowd''s reaction at Lystra.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (44, 15, 1, 'This passage leads into a meeting in Jerusalem, often called the Jerusalem Council, usually dated around AD 49.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (44, 16, 6, 'Near the end of this passage, after Paul''s vision at Troas, the account switches from ''they'' to ''we'', widely taken as the point where the writer joined Paul''s group.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (44, 16, 11, 'Philippi was a Roman colony in Macedonia, settled by retired soldiers, proud of its Roman status, and apparently with too few Jews for a synagogue.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (44, 17, 16, 'Athens was past its political glory but still a famous centre of philosophy; the Areopagus was its ancient council, named after the Hill of Ares.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (44, 18, 1, 'Emperor Claudius expelled Jews from Rome, usually dated around AD 49 though some place it earlier, and an inscription at Delphi dates Gallio''s time as governor to about AD 51–52.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (44, 19, 21, 'Ephesus was home to the vast temple of Artemis, one of the wonders of the ancient world, and a busy trade in religious souvenirs.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (44, 21, 27, 'Non-Jews were barred from the temple''s inner courts on pain of death, and Roman troops watched the crowds from the Antonia Fortress beside the temple.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (44, 21, 37, 'Josephus also mentions an Egyptian who led a large band of followers to the Mount of Olives around the 50s AD before Roman troops scattered them.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (44, 23, 23, 'Antonius Felix governed Judea from about AD 52 to 59; his wife Drusilla was a daughter of Herod Agrippa I.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (44, 25, 1, 'Porcius Festus replaced Felix around AD 59–60; Roman citizens could appeal to the emperor, at this time Nero.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (44, 25, 13, 'Agrippa II, son of Herod Agrippa I, ruled territories north of Judea; Bernice was his sister.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (44, 27, 9, '''The Fast'' is the Day of Atonement, in autumn; after mid-September Mediterranean sailing grew risky, and it largely stopped over winter.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (44, 28, 17, 'Paul''s two years under house arrest in Rome are usually dated around AD 60–62, during the reign of Nero.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (45, 2, 17, 'Paul writes here in a lively debating style familiar in his day, addressing an imagined questioner and answering objections, as teachers in the Greek world often did.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (45, 9, 1, 'By this time most of Paul''s fellow Jews had not accepted Jesus as the Messiah, while Gentiles were joining the churches in growing numbers.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (45, 11, 11, 'In this passage, Paul turns to speak directly to the Gentile believers in Rome, drawing on olive growing and grafting, a familiar part of Mediterranean farming life.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (45, 13, 1, 'Written under Nero''s early rule; the Roman historian Tacitus records public complaints in Rome about tax collectors around AD 58.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (45, 14, 1, 'The Roman believers disagreed over food and the keeping of special days. Jewish food laws and holy days were likely part of this, and possibly also worries about meat that had been offered in pagan temples before being sold in markets.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (45, 15, 22, 'Paul was about to take the gift from churches in Macedonia and Achaia (northern and southern Greece) to Jerusalem; Acts records he was arrested there and reached Rome later as a prisoner.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (45, 16, 1, 'Phoebe, from Cenchreae, Corinth''s port, probably carried the letter; Prisca (also called Priscilla) and Aquila had earlier been expelled from Rome under Claudius. A few scholars think this chapter was first sent elsewhere.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (45, 16, 21, 'In these closing greetings, Tertius, who wrote the letter down, adds his own greeting; an inscription found in Corinth names an official called Erastus, possibly the city treasurer mentioned here.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (46, 1, 1, 'Sosthenes may be the synagogue leader of that name who, according to Acts, was beaten in Corinth while Paul was there, though this is not certain.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (46, 1, 10, 'Paul had heard from members of Chloe''s household that the church was splitting into groups loyal to different leaders, including Paul, Apollos and Cephas (Peter).');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (46, 2, 1, 'Paul looks back to his first arrival in Corinth around AD 50, which according to Acts came straight after his visit to Athens.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (46, 3, 5, 'Apollos was an educated, eloquent Jewish Christian from Alexandria in Egypt who taught in Corinth after Paul had moved on.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (46, 5, 1, 'Paul had received a report of a church member living with his stepmother, a relationship forbidden under both Jewish and Roman law.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (46, 5, 9, 'Paul refers to an earlier letter he had written to Corinth, which has not survived.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (46, 6, 1, 'Some church members were taking one another to the city''s public courts, where Roman magistrates heard civil disputes.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (46, 6, 12, 'Many scholars think Paul is quoting slogans that were popular among some Corinthian believers, then responding to them.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (46, 7, 1, 'Here Paul begins answering questions the Corinthians had sent him in a letter, starting with questions about marriage.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (46, 8, 1, 'Paul answers their question about meat from animals sacrificed to idols, which was sold in markets and served at meals in temple dining rooms.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (46, 9, 1, 'Some in Corinth questioned whether Paul was a true apostle, partly because he supported himself by his trade instead of accepting payment.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (46, 9, 24, 'Corinth hosted the Isthmian Games every two years, a major athletic festival where winners received a simple wreath of leaves.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (46, 11, 17, 'The Lord''s Supper was then part of a shared meal in a home, and the wealthier members were apparently eating before poorer members arrived.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (46, 11, 23, 'This is the earliest written account of Jesus'' Last Supper, set down before the Gospels were written.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (46, 12, 1, 'Paul turns to another question from the Corinthians'' letter, this time about spiritual gifts and how they were being used in worship.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (46, 15, 1, 'Paul passes on an early summary of the faith he had received, including the oldest written list of people who saw the risen Jesus.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (46, 15, 12, 'Some in the church were saying the dead would not be raised, an idea that fit common Greek thinking, which often looked down on the body.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (46, 15, 29, 'Nobody now knows exactly what the practice of being baptised for the dead was, and scholars have offered many different explanations.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (46, 16, 1, 'Paul was organising a collection from his churches for poor believers in Jerusalem, which he planned to have delivered there.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (46, 16, 10, 'Timothy was Paul''s younger co-worker, whom he had sent ahead to visit Corinth.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (46, 16, 19, 'Aquila and Priscilla, a tentmaking couple who had worked alongside Paul in Corinth, were now with him in Ephesus, in the Roman province of Asia.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (47, 1, 1, 'Paul names Timothy, his younger co-worker, as co-sender, and addresses believers throughout Achaia as well as the church in Corinth.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (47, 1, 3, 'Later in this passage, Paul mentions that he had recently faced deadly danger in the province of Asia, in today''s western Turkey; the exact event is unknown, though some link it to trouble in Ephesus.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (47, 1, 15, 'Paul had changed his announced travel plans to visit Corinth, and some there accused him of being unreliable.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (47, 1, 23, 'After a painful visit, Paul sent a sorrowful letter instead of coming again; it is probably lost, though some think chapters 10–13 preserve it.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (47, 2, 5, 'One church member had caused Paul and the church serious hurt and had been disciplined by the congregation; who he was is not known.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (47, 2, 12, 'Troas was a port in northwest Asia Minor; Paul waited there for Titus to bring news from Corinth, then crossed to Macedonia.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (47, 2, 14, 'Paul draws on the Roman triumph, a grand victory parade through Rome for a conquering general, with his soldiers, captured prisoners led along the route, and sweet-smelling incense burning as the procession passed.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (47, 3, 7, 'This section recalls Moses'' face shining after he received the law on Mount Sinai, when he covered it with a veil.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (47, 4, 7, 'Clay jars were cheap, everyday pottery, commonly used in the ancient world for storing goods and even valuables.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (47, 6, 14, 'Corinth was full of temples to Greek and Roman gods; some scholars think this section came from another letter because it seems to interrupt the surrounding flow.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (47, 7, 2, 'Later in this passage, Paul tells how Titus finally met him in Macedonia, bringing news of how the Corinthians had received his sorrowful letter.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (47, 8, 1, 'The Macedonian churches, such as those at Philippi and Thessalonica, were themselves poor and under pressure, yet had given generously to the Jerusalem collection.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (47, 8, 16, 'Paul sent Titus with two unnamed fellow believers, one chosen by the churches, to oversee the collection openly and honestly.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (47, 9, 1, 'Paul had told the Macedonians that the believers of Achaia had been preparing their gift since the previous year.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (47, 10, 1, 'Critics in Corinth said Paul was forceful in letters but unimpressive in person; the sharper tone here leads many to see chapters 10–13 as a separate letter.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (47, 11, 1, 'Paul answers visiting teachers he calls super-apostles, impressive speakers who boasted of their credentials and probably had Jewish backgrounds.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (47, 11, 7, 'Paul refused payment from the Corinthians, working instead and accepting help from Macedonia, though in Greek culture respected teachers usually accepted patrons'' support.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (47, 11, 16, 'Paul''s escape from Damascus in a basket happened in the late 30s AD, when an official of King Aretas IV of Nabatea, an Arab kingdom to the south, was watching the city to arrest him.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (47, 12, 1, 'Paul describes a vision from fourteen years earlier, around the early 40s AD, and an ongoing affliction whose exact nature has never been known.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (47, 12, 14, 'Paul was preparing for his third visit to Corinth, after his founding stay and the later painful visit.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (48, 1, 11, 'Later in this passage Paul mentions going to Arabia soon after his conversion near Damascus. This probably means the Nabataean kingdom, east of the Jordan River and south of Damascus.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (48, 1, 18, 'In this passage Paul describes two visits to Jerusalem: a first to meet Peter and James, Jesus'' brother, and a second some years later, told in chapter 2. Scholars debate whether that second visit matches the church leaders'' meeting in Jerusalem around AD 49 or an earlier visit to bring famine relief.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (48, 2, 11, 'Antioch in Syria was a large city with one of the first churches mixing Jews and non-Jews, where shared meals raised questions about Jewish food laws.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (48, 4, 12, 'Paul recalls his first visit to the Galatians, when some illness or physical weakness led to his preaching among them.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (48, 6, 11, 'Paul often dictated his letters to a scribe; here he takes the pen himself to write the closing lines in large letters.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (49, 1, 1, 'Some of the earliest manuscripts lack the words naming Ephesus here, one reason many think this letter was copied and passed between several churches.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (49, 2, 11, 'In Jerusalem''s temple a stone barrier, with signs threatening death, kept non-Jews out of the inner courts. Some readers think the letter has this barrier in mind; others think it refers to the religious law that set Jews apart. The Romans destroyed the temple in AD 70.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (49, 5, 21, 'Greek and Roman writers often set out the duties within a household: husbands and wives, parents and children, masters and slaves. Slavery was widespread throughout the empire.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (49, 6, 10, 'Roman soldiers were a familiar sight across the empire; if Paul wrote under house arrest, a soldier may have guarded him as he wrote.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (49, 6, 21, 'Tychicus, a trusted companion from the province of Asia, is also named in Colossians as carrying news, and probably delivered this letter.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (50, 1, 1, 'Paul greets all the believers in Philippi, together with the church''s overseers (leaders) and deacons (helpers who served practical needs), an early glimpse of how local churches were organised.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (50, 1, 12, 'Paul reports that his imprisonment had become known among the guard, possibly the emperor''s elite Praetorian Guard, while other preachers, some rivals, spoke freely.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (50, 1, 19, 'Paul writes while awaiting a court verdict that could mean either release or execution.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (50, 1, 27, 'Later in this passage, many scholars think the lines in 2:6–11 come from an early Christian hymn or poem, perhaps already sung in churches before Paul wrote.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (50, 2, 19, 'Timothy, from Lystra in modern Turkey, had travelled with Paul for years and helped found the Philippian church; Paul planned to send him there.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (50, 2, 25, 'Epaphroditus was the Philippians'' own messenger who brought their gift; he fell gravely ill, nearly died, and Paul was now sending him home.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (50, 3, 1, 'Paul warns against opponents, most likely teachers who insisted non-Jewish believers be circumcised and keep Jewish law, and recounts his own background as a Pharisee from the tribe of Benjamin.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (50, 3, 12, 'Later in this passage, in 4:2, Paul names two women, Euodia and Syntyche, who had worked alongside him in Philippi but were now in disagreement.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (50, 4, 10, 'Paul thanks the Philippians for the gift Epaphroditus brought, recalling that they alone had supported him earlier, including while he was in Thessalonica.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (50, 4, 21, 'Greetings come from believers in the emperor''s household, meaning slaves and freed servants working in imperial service rather than the emperor''s family.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (51, 1, 3, 'Epaphras, a local man from Colossae, had first brought the Christian message there and had come to Paul with news of the church.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (51, 1, 15, 'Many scholars think this section may be an early Christian hymn or poem, possibly already sung in worship, which the writer included here.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (51, 1, 24, 'Paul writes from prison and mentions believers in nearby Laodicea too, churches he had never visited in person.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (51, 2, 6, 'This section answers teachers in Colossae promoting a religious philosophy built on human traditions; exactly who they were and what they taught is still debated.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (51, 2, 16, 'Some were pressing the church about food rules, festivals, new moons, Sabbath days and worship of angels, likely a blend of Jewish practices and local spiritual ideas.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (51, 3, 18, 'This follows the pattern of ''household rules'' common in Greek and Roman moral writing, addressing husbands, wives, children, fathers, slaves and masters in one home.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (51, 4, 7, 'Tychicus carried the letter with Onesimus, the enslaved man also named in Paul''s letter to Philemon. Near the end, the letter also mentions a letter to Laodicea; most think it has been lost, though some suggest it may be another surviving letter such as Ephesians.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (52, 1, 1, 'Silvanus is the Silas who travelled with Paul in the book of Acts; Timothy was a younger co-worker. Both had helped found the church in Thessalonica.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (52, 1, 2, 'News of the Thessalonians'' new faith had spread through Macedonia and Achaia, the Roman provinces covering northern and southern Greece.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (52, 2, 1, 'Paul arrived after being beaten and jailed in Philippi, and travelling speakers who sought money and praise were common, so he recalls working to support himself.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (52, 2, 13, 'The believers were suffering at the hands of their own townspeople; a minority of scholars think part of this section was added later, though most accept it as Paul''s.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (52, 2, 17, 'After being forced out of Thessalonica, Paul went on to Athens and, anxious for the church, sent Timothy back from there to strengthen them.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (52, 3, 6, 'Timothy had just rejoined Paul, likely in Corinth, bringing an encouraging report about the church; this news seems to have prompted the letter.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (52, 4, 1, 'These converts came from a Greek and Roman culture where sexual relationships outside marriage, especially for men, were widely tolerated.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (52, 4, 9, 'Many Greeks looked down on manual labour, and it seems some in the church had stopped working and were relying on others.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (52, 4, 13, 'Some believers had died since Paul left, and the church was troubled about what this meant for them when Jesus returned.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (52, 5, 23, 'Letters like this were read aloud to the whole congregation gathered together, often in someone''s home.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (53, 1, 3, 'This section speaks to believers who were still enduring persecution and hardship from people around them in Thessalonica.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (53, 2, 1, 'Some in the church had been alarmed by claims, possibly even a letter falsely said to be from Paul, that the ''day of the Lord'' (the long-awaited day when God would act decisively and Jesus would return) had already arrived.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (53, 3, 6, 'This addresses some church members who had stopped working and were living off the generosity of others.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (53, 3, 14, 'Letters were usually dictated to a scribe; near the end of this passage the writer adds a closing greeting in his own handwriting as a mark that the letter is genuine.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (54, 1, 3, 'Paul had urged Timothy to stay on in Ephesus while Paul travelled to Macedonia in northern Greece, a journey that does not fit neatly into the travels recorded in Acts.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (54, 1, 18, 'Paul names two men, Hymenaeus and Alexander, probably known to Timothy in Ephesus, whose faith had collapsed; a Hymenaeus is mentioned again in 2 Timothy.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (54, 3, 1, '"Overseer" translates a Greek word for a local church leader; Acts describes Paul meeting the leaders of the Ephesus church earlier at the nearby port of Miletus.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (54, 3, 14, 'Paul writes hoping to visit Timothy soon, but sends these instructions in case he is delayed.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (54, 4, 1, 'This section warns against teachers, probably already active in Ephesus, who told believers to avoid marriage and certain foods.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (54, 4, 11, 'Timothy was young by the standards of the day to be guiding older believers, and church leaders had earlier laid hands on him (placing their hands on him while praying) to commission him.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (54, 5, 3, 'With no state support, widows without family often faced poverty, and the Ephesus church kept a list of widows it supported.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (54, 6, 1, 'Some members of the church were slaves, and some of them served masters who were themselves believers.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (54, 6, 17, 'Ephesus was a wealthy trading city, and the church there included some well-off members.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (55, 1, 15, 'Asia here means the Roman province around Ephesus in western Turkey; Onesiphorus apparently came from Ephesus and searched Rome to find the imprisoned Paul.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (55, 3, 1, 'Jannes and Jambres are names Jewish tradition gave to Pharaoh''s magicians who opposed Moses; the book of Exodus itself does not name them.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (55, 3, 10, 'Pisidian Antioch (not the larger Antioch in Syria), Iconium and Lystra were towns in south-central Turkey where Paul was attacked on his first missionary journey; Lystra was probably Timothy''s hometown.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (55, 4, 6, 'The letter pictures Paul expecting execution soon; early church tradition holds that he was beheaded outside Rome during Nero''s reign.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (55, 4, 9, 'The "first defence" probably means an initial hearing before a Roman court; Dalmatia was a region on the coast of modern Croatia.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (55, 4, 19, 'Priscilla and Aquila were a tentmaking married couple who had worked alongside Paul in Corinth and Ephesus years earlier.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (56, 1, 5, 'Titus had been left on Crete to appoint elders, trusted church leaders also called overseers, in each town, where churches seem to have lacked formal leadership.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (56, 1, 10, 'The troublemakers included Jewish-background teachers pressing Jewish law and myths; the insulting line about Cretans is traditionally credited to Epimenides, a Cretan poet of around 600 BC.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (56, 2, 1, 'Early churches met in homes whose households included older and younger members and slaves, and Greek moral writers of the day often gave guidance arranged by household role.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (56, 3, 12, 'Nicopolis was probably the city in western Greece where Paul planned to winter; he meant to send Artemas or Tychicus to Crete so Titus could leave and join him, while Zenas the lawyer and Apollos were travelling through, possibly carrying the letter.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (57, 1, 1, 'Besides Philemon, the greeting names Apphia and Archippus, probably members of his household, and the church that met in his home, so the letter was likely read aloud there.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (57, 1, 8, 'The name Onesimus meant "useful", a common slave name, and Paul plays on it; Onesimus was likely travelling back carrying this letter, possibly with Paul''s helper Tychicus.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (57, 1, 17, 'Letters were usually dictated to a scribe, so Paul writing a line in his own hand made his promise to repay any loss a personally signed pledge.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (57, 1, 22, 'Paul expected to be released soon; the companions he names, including Epaphras, Mark and Luke, also appear in Colossians, linking the two letters.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (58, 3, 7, 'The writer draws on Psalm 95, which recalls Israel complaining in the desert after leaving Egypt, a generation that never entered the promised land of Canaan.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (58, 5, 11, 'This section addresses readers who had been believers for some time but, in the writer''s view, had stalled and grown sluggish in understanding.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (58, 6, 13, 'This passage opens with God''s promise to Abraham, then turns to Melchizedek, a priest-king of Salem, probably early Jerusalem, who blessed Abraham. Melchizedek appears only briefly in Genesis and in Psalm 110.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (58, 9, 1, 'The writer describes the tabernacle, the portable worship tent of Moses'' day, and the yearly Day of Atonement, when the high priest entered its innermost room.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (58, 12, 18, 'The scene recalls Mount Sinai, where Israel received God''s law amid fire, darkness and trembling, alongside Mount Zion, the hill of Jerusalem, which the writer pictures here as a heavenly city.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (58, 13, 7, 'The leaders remembered here were probably the community''s first teachers, possibly now dead, while its current leaders are mentioned separately later.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (58, 13, 18, 'The closing mentions Timothy, Paul''s younger co-worker, who had recently been released, probably from prison. The writer also passes on greetings from believers from Italy.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (59, 2, 1, 'Early believers met in gatherings James calls by the Greek word for ''synagogue'', where rich and poor visitors could arrive side by side.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (59, 2, 14, 'Many scholars think this section answers people who had misunderstood Paul''s teaching about faith; others think James wrote independently of Paul.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (59, 4, 13, 'Travelling merchants were common in the Roman world, moving between towns to trade for a season or a year.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (59, 5, 1, 'In this period wealthy landowners often hired day labourers for harvest, and workers depended on being paid each evening to feed their families.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (59, 5, 7, 'Farmers in the land of Israel depended on autumn rains to soften the ground for planting and spring rains to ripen the crops.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (59, 5, 13, 'Later in this passage James mentions the elders, the recognised leaders of a local church. In his day olive oil was a common medicine, and anointing someone with oil was also a familiar religious act.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (60, 2, 11, 'Christians were often accused of being troublemakers, and this section addresses life under the emperor and the provincial governors who kept order for Rome.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (60, 2, 18, 'Roman households expected slaves and wives to follow the master''s religion; this section addresses household slaves, wives (some of them married to husbands who were not believers) and husbands.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (60, 4, 12, 'The label "Christian" was still new, often used by outsiders as an insult; scholars debate whether this suffering was local hostility or official persecution.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (60, 5, 1, 'Elders were the older, trusted leaders who looked after each local congregation; the writer speaks to them as a fellow elder.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (60, 5, 12, 'Silvanus is probably the Silas who travelled with Paul; Mark is probably the John Mark who appears in the book of Acts and travelled for a time with Paul, and whom early tradition links with the Gospel of Mark; and "Babylon" is widely understood as a code name for Rome.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (61, 1, 12, 'Written as a farewell, Peter speaks of his approaching death and recalls being on the mountain when Jesus appeared in shining glory, an event called the transfiguration.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (61, 2, 1, 'This section closely resembles the short letter of Jude; most scholars think 2 Peter drew on Jude, though some argue the borrowing went the other way.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (61, 3, 1, 'The writer calls this his second letter, most likely pointing back to 1 Peter, and turns to scoffers who doubted Jesus would ever return.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (61, 3, 14, 'Later in this passage the writer mentions Paul''s letters, one of the earliest signs that they were already being gathered, circulated among churches, and read alongside the Jewish scriptures.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (62, 1, 1, 'Unlike most letters of the time, it opens with no greeting or named sender, and its first lines closely echo the opening of John''s Gospel.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (62, 2, 18, 'Here the letter speaks directly about the split: a group had recently walked out of the fellowship, and the writer addresses the believers they left behind.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (62, 4, 1, 'Travelling teachers moved between early churches, and the dispute here seems to concern those who denied that Jesus truly came in a real human body.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (63, 1, 7, 'This passage concerns the travelling teachers who claimed that Jesus had not truly come as a flesh-and-blood human. Later in the passage the elder turns to hospitality: welcoming a travelling teacher into one''s home, often where the church met, was seen as publicly backing their message.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (63, 1, 12, 'The letter is short enough to fit a single sheet of papyrus, a common writing material, written on in ink. Letters were carried by hand, and church leaders often travelled to visit congregations in person. The "chosen sister" mentioned at the end of the passage is probably the church where the elder lived, or, if the "lady" was a real woman, perhaps her actual sister.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (64, 1, 5, 'Gaius had been housing travelling Christian workers who refused support from non-believers, and some had returned to report his kindness to the elder''s church.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (64, 1, 9, 'This answers trouble from Diotrephes, a local church leader who rejected the elder''s earlier letter, refused his messengers, and expelled members who welcomed them.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (64, 1, 12, 'Demetrius, whom the elder commends, was probably the one carrying this letter to Gaius.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (65, 1, 5, 'In this passage Jude reminds his readers of stories they already knew. Some come from the Old Testament, such as Israel''s escape from Egypt. Others come from popular Jewish writings outside the Old Testament: later in the passage he mentions a tradition about the archangel Michael disputing over the body of Moses, and words from 1 Enoch, an ancient Jewish book written in the name of Enoch, the early ancestor listed in Genesis.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (66, 1, 9, 'Patmos was a small, rocky island in the Aegean Sea off the coast of Asia; John was there, most likely as an exile, because of his preaching.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (66, 2, 1, 'Ephesus was the province''s greatest city and a major port, home to the famous temple of the goddess Artemis; Paul had worked there for over two years.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (66, 2, 8, 'Smyrna was a wealthy port city, proud of its long loyalty to Rome and an early temple to the emperor; the church there was poor and facing hostility.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (66, 2, 12, 'Pergamum, a former royal capital on a high hill, had a huge altar to Zeus, a healing shrine, and the province''s first temple honouring the Roman emperor.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (66, 2, 18, 'Thyatira was a trading town known for its craft guilds, such as dyers and bronze workers; guild banquets usually honoured pagan gods. Lydia, Paul''s convert, came from here.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (66, 3, 1, 'Sardis, once the rich capital of the kingdom of Lydia, had a hilltop fortress twice captured by surprise; it was rebuilt after a severe earthquake in AD 17.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (66, 3, 7, 'Philadelphia was a smaller city on an important trade road, badly damaged by the AD 17 earthquake and shaken by tremors for years afterwards.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (66, 3, 14, 'Laodicea was a wealthy banking city known for black wool and eye ointment; its water was piped in from a distance, and nearby Hierapolis had hot springs.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (66, 13, 11, 'Near the end of this passage a number appears. In the ancient world letters of the alphabet also served as numbers, so adding up the value of a name was a familiar kind of puzzle.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (66, 17, 7, 'Later in this passage seven hills, or mountains, are mentioned. In the ancient world Rome was famously known as the city built on seven hills.');
insert into public.bible_pericope_settings (book_number, start_chapter, start_verse, note) values (66, 18, 9, 'Later in this passage merchants list the cargoes they once sold. Rome drew luxury goods such as silk, spices, ivory and fine wood from across the empire and beyond, much of it shipped by sea through busy ports.');
